WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.800
You're looking at a price of Bitcoin per coin of about 700,000 US dollars.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.320
The price of Bitcoin is extremely low right now compared to what it will be in the future.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.880
It's a real pain to have to go and pay someone 2% every time you land in a new airport to get access to their currency.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.640
There are people in power that are benefiting from this 2% that you're talking about.

5
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.280
Without a doubt, I don't think that the next reserve currency of the world is going to be associated with the country.

6
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.320
This is like a miracle.

7
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.880
We're kind of living in the future but with a lot of the braces and the belts of the past.

8
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.040
Does it make sense that because I have a United States passport because I was born in California,

9
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.160
that I have all these rights?

10
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.200
Whereas a kid that was born the same day as me but was born a few kilometers south in Mexico

11
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.560
is now doesn't have these rights because they were born in a different plot of land.

12
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.200
Over the last few years, humanity has almost got into a stage where

13
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.920
we've forgotten how to get along with people that had different views.

14
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.200
There's a barrier to people understanding crypto is they don't understand money.

15
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.600
Forget about crypto, they just don't understand money.

16
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.880
And gosh, I'm giving my funds to the bank so that the bank can use my funds to lend out and make money on those funds.

17
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.360
And I'm paying the bank for the privilege of then using my money.

18
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.800
In Africa, when you send money from South Africa to Zimbabwe, there's a big remittance corridor.

19
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.400
A lot of Zimbabweans are living in South Africa.

20
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:13.120
They're sending money back.

21
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.200
Pretty much the standard fee that people are paying for that privilege is 10%.

22
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.480
Wow.

23
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.560
I think there's a tremendous amount of injustice there where you have the intermediaries that are

24
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.040
making a lot of money off the back of a lot of poor people around the world.

25
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.120
The inflation hits the poor the most.

26
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.240
In South Africa and other places of Africa, there's a lot of poverty.

27
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.960
If you're driving from one place to another, you're going to pass a lot of people that haven't eaten.

28
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.880
For Bitcoin to become a little bit more prevalent in the world, I think we're going to have to see

29
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.000
some crises in our traditional financial system.

30
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.680
The US dollar cannot exist forever.

31
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.800
So if you go from a world where the US dollar exists and it's the dominant currency and it's

32
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:57.840
the reserve asset to a world where it doesn't exist anymore, that transition has to be tumultuous.

33
00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.880
Change is inevitable.

34
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:00.720
It really is inevitable.

35
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.080
And we have a difficult time perceiving that as humans when you're in a particular society,

36
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.360
in a particular paradigm.

37
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.720
But when paradigm shifts come, they can change very, very quickly.

38
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.920
There are oftentimes huge shifts that are sudden that change the nature of power in the world,

39
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.520
that change the nature of wealth in the world.

40
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.240
The price of gold altogether is probably between, or the value of all gold that exists right now

41
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.760
in the world is anywhere between let's say 12 to 17 trillion US dollars.

42
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.640
Anything that has digital, that is digital, that has kind of gone on to replace its analog version,

43
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.280
has not just gone to match the value of that analog version, but has gone and exceeded it

44
00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.560
by many orders of magnitude.

45
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.320
We're living in a dog-eat-dog world.

46
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.760
I think a lot of the crypto assets that exist today will go to zero.

47
00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.600
Crypto is not for everybody at the moment.

48
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.240
Trust me.

92
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.060
She's the queen of the game.

93
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.380
Stacking crypto like it's her claim to fame.

94
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.400
If you want success, then take a look away.

95
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.160
Hi, Farzam.

96
00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.800
Hello, Bonnie. How are you doing?

97
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.820
Great. It's so good to have you here.

98
00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.060
You were born in the US, grew up in Kenya,

99
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:12.160
and now you run a very successful crypto exchange called Valor in South Africa.

100
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:17.140
And given that you've seen all these different cultures, here's my question.

101
00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:21.780
Some people are pushing this idea of having a global currency.

102
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.360
Do you think we need a global currency?

103
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.620
And does it make more sense to have different currencies for different countries?

104
00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:32.160
So I think we definitely need a global currency for the world.

105
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.880
Ultimately, we're heading towards that.

106
00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.640
But there's going to be quite a transition to make that happen.

107
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.560
And I think all you need to do is look back in time,

108
00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:42.720
the history of our money.

109
00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:47.080
And you realize it wasn't that long ago that we didn't even have national currencies.

110
00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.860
In many countries around the world, the United States, South Africa, other places,

111
00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.700
banks themselves issued their own currency.

112
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.860
And so the currency that we had was just receipts of, let's say, gold that was deposited at a bank.

113
00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.860
And depending on how that particular bank was doing,

114
00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.060
would depend on how or would determine how well the money that you had was doing.

115
00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:12.880
And then now it's kind of standard that each country or region has its own currency.

116
00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:22.660
But I think ultimately it's a real pain to have to go and pay someone 2% every time you land in a new airport to get access to their currency.

117
00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.000
So if I were to tell you in two different cities, City A and City B in Taiwan,

118
00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:33.060
that you had to go and exchange your money in one city for the currency of another city,

119
00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:40.020
or even worse, from one neighborhood in your city to another neighborhood currency in your city,

120
00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.660
that would be a real pain.

121
00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.280
And that's kind of the way that we are in the world right now.

122
00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.300
It's a no-brainer to me that we are moving towards that.

123
00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.320
It will take some time.

124
00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.340
There will be some huge adjustments in my view.

125
00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:59.120
But the world not only needs a world currency, but it deserves a world currency in time.

126
00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:04.380
But I feel like there are people in power that are benefiting from this 2% that you're talking about.

127
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.160
Without a doubt.

128
00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:06.780
And I think that's the story of humanity.

129
00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.420
You always have vested interests.

130
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.200
And vested interests want to hold on to the power that they have so that they can benefit from the status quo.

131
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:25.580
But I think the story of human progress is something that has always happened and will continue to happen against the vested interests of that time.

132
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.000
And that's what I talk about.

133
00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:32.000
I think there will be some struggles between the vested interests and the status quo and progress.

134
00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:37.580
But I think progress ultimately prevails when you look and zoom out enough.

135
00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.500
Progress prevails for humanity.

136
00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:44.520
So I'm a very big optimist about where we're going for humanity.

137
00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.900
I do think, as I said, there will be some bumps along the way.

138
00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:50.040
But ultimately, the people will prevail.

139
00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.000
Do you think Bitcoin will be the obvious choice for this global currency?

140
00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.520
I think we have to have some humility, right?

141
00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.240
And to say, okay, what are the options?

142
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.700
Up until now, obviously, the United States dollar is the reserve currency of the world.

143
00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.140
Before that, we had different reserve currency.

144
00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.100
We had the pound and we had, you know, currencies from Spain and from Netherlands.

145
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.960
And if you looked at different epochs of history, different reserve currencies of the world.

146
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.660
I don't think that the next reserve currency of the world is going to be associated with the country.

147
00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.780
And I think there are a few reasons for that.

148
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:22.740
We're living in it.

149
00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.540
But the fact that you're in Taiwan, I'm right now in Hong Kong.

150
00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:26.680
We didn't even know.

151
00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:27.840
I didn't know where you were.

152
00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.160
And we're having a seamless conversation with this thing called the internet and just this video chat.

153
00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:34.560
We take that for granted now.

154
00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:35.920
But this is like a miracle.

155
00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.860
This is really we're living in the technological future, right?

156
00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.660
The internet has brought us all together.

157
00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:52.140
This mechanism of intercommunication across the entire human race is such that we're kind of living in the future, but with a lot of the braces and the belts of the past, right?

158
00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.020
And so what I mean by that is the notion of a nation state.

159
00:06:55.020 --> 00:07:03.480
The notion that this determines so much of where we can go as human beings and what our rights are and what taxes we have to pay, etc.

160
00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:10.740
That notion is very different to what we're currently living in in this kind of borderless society that's been enabled through the internet.

161
00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:14.120
Historically, we didn't have much communication transfer.

162
00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.320
Only in the last two, three decades has there been an explosion of information across the internet.

163
00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:24.760
And so right now, when I grew up in Kenya, I had a very Kenyan-specific upbringing.

164
00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:25.560
What does that mean?

165
00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:26.980
So I'll give you an example.

166
00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:30.380
Like there wasn't a lot of materialism in Kenya.

167
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:31.600
It was a very poor nation.

168
00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:37.360
We didn't have tons of toys or tons of options in the grocery stores and things of that nature.

169
00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:45.520
Kids in America, and it's just a very different kind of narrative that was there in Kenya, a very different narrative to the one in the United States when I was growing up.

170
00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.460
Now, the narrative for a lot of the kids around the world is what they're seeing on social media.

171
00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:54.520
And a lot of the kids, unfortunately, in my view, are getting onto social media quite early.

172
00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.300
But the point is, is that there's a narrative that's global because of this intercommunication that we have.

173
00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.760
And so the reason I bring that up is that we're living in a global village.

174
00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:16.060
And that global village, I don't think it makes any sense for the future to have a world currency that is under the power of a particular subset of humanity in a particular country.

175
00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:20.080
So I think the next global currency will be that.

176
00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:25.260
It will be a global currency outside of the purview of just a subset of humanity.

177
00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.680
And I think it could be gold.

178
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.060
I don't think we're going to the back to the gold standard.

179
00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.920
And so I asked myself, well, what else is it?

180
00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:34.680
I think fiat currency is on the decline.

181
00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:39.120
And so I think Bitcoin has a very big chance of being that.

182
00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.480
But I think whether it's Bitcoin or not, it's going to be based on cryptography.

183
00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.180
So I think it will be some type of a crypto asset.

184
00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.320
It may have not existed or it may not have been invented yet.

185
00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:48.920
I don't know.

186
00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:55.940
But I do think that we're moving to this world where we have a currency for the world, for humanity as a whole.

187
00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.100
The economists that are watching this will start to say, what is this guy talking about?

188
00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:06.120
We need to have different currencies so they can devalue one currency against another, boost their exports and things of that nature.

189
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:07.940
But I think it comes down to identity.

190
00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:14.860
We don't talk about California seceding and having its own currency so that it can boost export if it's not doing as well.

191
00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:16.940
You know, versus the other parts of the United States.

192
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:21.980
So I think this idea of identity and money goes hand in hand.

193
00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.520
And I think more and more, we're identifying as a human race.

194
00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:29.560
And we're going to go through these challenges that we have with identity.

195
00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:36.720
But ultimately, I think we're moving towards just realizing that we're one human family and we'll need a currency to go with that.

196
00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:40.860
There were discussions about whether certain countries should be a part of EU, right?

197
00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:44.260
Because your economy sucks and mine is strong.

198
00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:52.000
And if you expand that to a global scale, powerful countries like the US might be like, hey, you are too small of a country.

199
00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:55.380
And I don't want to be using the same currency as yours.

200
00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.980
I think that's what we're seeing right now.

201
00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:00.960
The reason I brought up my example before is that right now we have the euro.

202
00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:11.660
Remember about 10 years ago, 13 years ago, when there was the euro debt crisis, a lot of the people in Europe were saying, listen, you know, Greece has a lot of the troubles.

203
00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:14.000
They should go back to the Greek drachma.

204
00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:18.620
They should not have the euro so that they can devalue their currency and they can kind of reset.

205
00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:23.760
It doesn't make sense for them to have such a strong currency like the euro, like the rest of us, and particularly Germany.

206
00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.540
But again, that comes down to identity.

207
00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:32.720
And so that's why I mean that humanity is kind of this adolescent phase and we're finally heading towards maturity.

208
00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:48.480
But if you use that analogy of adolescence into maturity as human beings, we're going to have a whole bunch of pimples and, you know, just ugliness that's going to come and like trying to figure out who we are as human beings and youth going into adulthood.

209
00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:57.500
I see a maturity of the human race at a stage where we recognize the oneness of the human race, regardless of what particular piece of dirt we're born on.

210
00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:03.600
But there are going to be some challenges along the way, you know, because of the vested interests that we talked a little bit earlier about.

211
00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.580
It sounds like you're very optimistic.

212
00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.440
Yes, so it's definitely true.

213
00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:12.900
But I don't think it's just a dream of like kind of world peace and world unity.

214
00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.140
I think it's inevitable.

215
00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:15.780
I think it will have to happen.

216
00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:21.740
And the reason I say that is the last, say, 15, 16 years of my life was spent in South Africa.

217
00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:27.360
And South Africa up until 1994 was an apartheid nation, completely segregated by race.

218
00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.060
Now, of course, that's totally unacceptable.

219
00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:34.480
The country is not segregated by race anymore, you know, from a legal perspective.

220
00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:37.040
Obviously, from a social perspective, there's a lot of progress.

221
00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:38.400
Let's just take a look at this.

222
00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:44.900
Like right now, we've got yourself, a wonderful woman who is interviewing me, a man.

223
00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:51.160
A hundred years ago, 200 years ago, this would be unthinkable because the status of women's society was not there.

224
00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.480
Like women were oppressed in society.

225
00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:57.800
And we've still got, I believe, a long way to go with the equality of men and women in society.

226
00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:00.940
But we've come a long way where we were 100, 200 years ago.

227
00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:06.300
Remember, just over 100 years ago, women in the United States didn't even have the right to vote.

228
00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:10.940
So the point is, when you look at that perspective, then you say, OK, we've made a lot of progress.

229
00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:15.040
And does it make sense that we're so divided as a human race?

230
00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:21.240
Does it make sense that because I have a United States passport, because I was born in California, that I have all these rights?

231
00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:31.060
Whereas a kid that was born the same day as me, but was born a few kilometers south in Mexico, is now doesn't have these rights because they were born in a different plot of land?

232
00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:32.900
These things don't really make sense to me.

233
00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:34.240
I'm not only optimistic.

234
00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:35.260
I think it's inevitable.

235
00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:42.440
But the progress to get there and the process to get there and the path to get there, I think is not going to be smooth.

236
00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:43.600
But we will get there.

237
00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:50.940
Talking about U.S. and Mexico, does that mean you believe in the future there will be one government in charge of the whole world?

238
00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:57.880
If you look at the history of our world again, we've come from those family units moved and became tribal units that used to feud.

239
00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:01.940
Those tribal units then became city-states that used to feud.

240
00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:08.280
Those city-states then became nation-states, which was in about the 1600s, which is where we currently are right now.

241
00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:19.760
So the next logical evolution in the way I see the world going is that we are going to be moving towards a world federation of nations, where, yes, we'll have our own cultures, we'll have different languages, etc.

242
00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.360
But I think there will be a world auxiliary language.

243
00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:29.440
I think there will be a world auxiliary economic system and currency system that will facilitate trade between us.

244
00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:40.860
And, yes, I do believe that the next stage of governance for the human race is going to be world governance, where the interests and the rights of humanity as a whole will be safeguarded.

245
00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:47.980
I think we'll have to go through quite a lot of change because our current governance systems are flawed in many ways.

246
00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:50.820
So I think we're going to be moving to new types of governance systems.

247
00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:57.800
But I do believe that ultimately we're going to have a world legislature, world judiciary, and I think we're going to have a world executive force.

248
00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:00.920
There's a grand vision there, but I think it's definitely coming.

249
00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:04.140
I remember a few years ago, people seemed a lot more welcoming.

250
00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:09.000
And I think this was pre-COVID, and it seemed like globalization was inevitable.

251
00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:14.620
But now it seems like the world has this strange mentality of us versus them.

252
00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:16.700
Why do you think that is?

253
00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:20.200
So I think transition comes with a lot of difficulty.

254
00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:30.740
Over the last few years, humanity has almost gotten to a stage where we've forgotten how to get along with people that had different views to ourselves.

255
00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:37.520
And we have these echo chambers, and it's actually started to divide families in the United States, or even COVID.

256
00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:41.180
COVID was dividing friends and families because of masks, as an example.

257
00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:43.180
Like, oh, I can't believe, or vaccines.

258
00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:47.760
Right now with political parties, like, are you part of that political party and not this political party?

259
00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:48.620
We can't even talk to you.

260
00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:50.440
Get a little bit philosophical with you, Bunny.

261
00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:55.640
Ultimately, this world is based on unity.

262
00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:58.160
And it is based on love.

263
00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:07.700
And what I mean by that is even if you go all the way down to the molecular level, what keeps this world together is the attraction between different parts of an atom.

264
00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:09.820
So at a physical level, there's attraction.

265
00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:15.400
At a societal level, I think what keeps us together is love, I think, and unity.

266
00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:20.060
And when love and unity is severed, then we start to have big problems.

267
00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:27.300
And I think right now we're at a stage where people are going through a kind of transition and they're realizing, oh, actually, I don't know if I like this.

268
00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:28.840
And I'm not so familiar with it.

269
00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:29.680
I'm not used to it.

270
00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:32.620
So let me try to go back and protect my rights and my people.

271
00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:37.940
And so they put up barriers and tariffs and all sorts of kind of divisions.

272
00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:45.320
But I think that we'll see that we are much weaker as society when we're divided than when we're united.

273
00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:49.020
Our own lives, we have crises and victories in our process of life.

274
00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:50.740
And society is no different.

275
00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:57.720
And so I think we're going to go through these crises and victories and that we'll have some crises, but we'll realize this and that was a bad idea.

276
00:15:57.900 --> 00:15:59.280
And so let's move in a different direction.

277
00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:07.280
But if you zoom out, this concept of unity is the prevailing concept of human society.

278
00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:11.280
You really scientifically cannot dispute that.

279
00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:16.860
You know, I'm a Baha'i, which is my a lot of my kind of belief systems come from the Baha'i faith.

280
00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:29.080
But if you've read Sapiens by Yuval Harari, he's obviously talking about the evolution of the world from a very atheistic and kind of non-spiritual dimension.

281
00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:42.320
Coming from both of these kind of spiritual and a non-spiritual perspective, the facts are kind of in tandem and synchronous, which is that we're moving to a more unified society ultimate.

282
00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:53.520
I think with anything there are challenges, but there is no doubt in my mind if you just take a step back and look at the facts of society and where we are in the world, that we're moving more and more towards a more unified world.

283
00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:55.200
And I think the world will be better for it.

284
00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:57.260
Can you tell us more about Baha'i?

285
00:16:57.260 --> 00:17:02.560
So the Baha'i faith is a world religion that started about 180-ish years ago.

286
00:17:02.740 --> 00:17:04.700
So it's a very young world religion.

287
00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:07.260
And its main point is that of unity.

288
00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:09.140
It talks about the unity of the human race.

289
00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:10.540
It talks about the unity of religion.

290
00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:15.960
It talks about the unity of the divine, whatever you want to call that, unknowable essence, right?

291
00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:21.920
We have lots of different words, God or Jehovah or Allah or whatever background you come from.

292
00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.660
And I think that's really based on the limitations that we place in our own language.

293
00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:30.840
If you take a step back and you kind of think about who are we and what are we doing over here?

294
00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:33.400
What can make sense of our reality?

295
00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:38.080
The Baha'i faith has very beautiful views of where we're going as humanity.

296
00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:44.340
And a lot of the things that I've shared with you today are based on my understanding of what the Baha'i vision is for where we're going as a world.

297
00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:50.060
Its number one objective is the establishment of peace and unity in the world.

298
00:17:50.280 --> 00:18:00.220
And it basically says that for humanity to reach the next stage of its rightful evolution, we need to become a united community, a global community.

299
00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:08.300
We need to set aside our differences and prejudices that have plagued us for the history of our evolution.

300
00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:18.360
And it really is a beautiful message and a rekindling of the messages of all the previous divine teachings of love.

301
00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:26.560
It talks about the fact that if you have a religious belief that is not backed by science, then it's probably superstitious.

302
00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:32.460
And if you have science that doesn't have the guidance of religion, then it can be very misguided as well.

303
00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:36.360
So there's a lot of beautiful kind of belief systems within the Baha'i faith.

304
00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:44.200
And it's the most, the single most beautiful prism of interpreting reality that I've ever come across.

305
00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:49.000
So I encourage listeners to look into it, read about it and decide for themselves.

306
00:18:49.000 --> 00:19:00.020
I went through all of your interviews online and I remember very vividly that you talk about there is this one truth and that even white lies are not acceptable.

307
00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:02.820
And this is very different from what I believe.

308
00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:04.400
And I'd like to know what you think.

309
00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:13.640
So I believe that people grow up very differently and everyone is from different families, different financial backgrounds, different cultures.

310
00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:18.680
So people look at the world very differently and it's all perspective.

311
00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:21.340
So help me understand here.

312
00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:25.020
How do you define what is the truth?

313
00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:30.700
When I talk about truth, so I really do believe that truthfulness is the foundation of all human virtues, right?

314
00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:35.560
That without truthfulness, it's very difficult to have any virtuous society.

315
00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:39.200
When I talk about truth, there's an objective reality of what happened.

316
00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:55.500
You know, different people may have different perspectives, but I think what we're seeing in the world right now is this kind of concept of relativism, which kind of everything goes and your perspective and your perception of reality, whatever it is, is fine.

317
00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:57.380
I don't really share that.

318
00:19:57.540 --> 00:20:00.760
I do believe that there are things that are right and there are things that are wrong.

319
00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:03.340
And I think as society, we believe that as well, right?

320
00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:08.220
We've come to believe that, you know, going and killing somebody is not okay, right?

321
00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:10.920
It's called murder and you can be put behind bars, etc.

322
00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:16.000
So, you know, if you make it very extreme, then there are certain things that we could probably agree on.

323
00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:18.820
In my spare time, I teach children's classes.

324
00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:32.380
I was talking to this mother of a three or four-year-old child and I was explaining to her how we run these groups and how it's really important to bring up the next generation to really understand what these virtues are so that we can make the world a better place.

325
00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:35.120
And she looked at me and she said, that sounds great.

326
00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:44.120
I'm really confused because the world has become such a crazy place and I don't know whether I should teach my kids that or whether I should teach my child to be clever.

327
00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:45.680
And I said, what do you mean by that?

328
00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:48.860
And she said, you know, we're living in a dog-eat-dog world.

329
00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:52.960
And so maybe I just need to teach my kid to survive.

330
00:20:53.240 --> 00:21:01.440
And what that means is maybe lie to get ahead or stab someone in the back to, you know, get their position.

331
00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:13.520
And I thought that's a really sad state of affairs because that's kind of where we came from, you know, in the origins of humanity where we were all kind of for our own needs, fighting for ourselves.

332
00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:20.600
There's no rule of law, but a society we've kind of evolved to realize that, you know, rule of law is actually good.

333
00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:22.000
So long as there's justice.

334
00:21:22.300 --> 00:21:31.680
And you can imagine that if the next generation of people were taught what she was thinking about teaching her children, we would be heading into a world that's even worse than what it is right now in many ways.

335
00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:38.100
So coming back to your question about what is truth, you know, there are spiritual conceptions of what truth is, but I think that gets too philosophical.

336
00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:45.340
But when I talk about truth and being truthful, what I mean is that if I talk to my finance manager and I said, what is this number?

337
00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:51.620
I expect to know the reality behind that number and not some made up story about what didn't happen.

338
00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:55.480
Because if I don't know the truth, then I can't take care of my customer funds, etc.

339
00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:56.500
So that's the type of truth.

340
00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:59.380
And I'm saying it needs to be representative of reality.

341
00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:05.300
But I feel like, okay, in crypto, people promote this mentality of don't trust, verify.

342
00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:16.280
And because people were traumatized by a lot of events that happened in 2022, for example, Luna, FTX, and a lot of numbers in crypto are manipulated.

343
00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:18.380
And I'm sure you've seen a lot of these examples.

344
00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:20.720
What is your take on this?

345
00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:26.060
So the don't trust, verify is, I think that's, there's a lot of health in that.

346
00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:28.660
Like where you can verify, you should verify.

347
00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:39.880
And that's coming from the context of a world where our trusted institutions are not so trustworthy anymore, right?

348
00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:55.940
Whether those trusted institutions are political parties or corporations or religious institutions or any other social institution that's meant to serve people, but is actually not doing that, but looking at their own interests.

349
00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:59.780
And so in that context, of course, it makes sense to say, listen, don't trust.

350
00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:05.760
People are not behaving as they should be doing, kind of with a loving mentality of how can I serve humanity?

351
00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:09.260
And as a result, then I need to verify because I don't know who I can trust.

352
00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:10.080
And I think that's true.

353
00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:11.360
Like we all have to do that.

354
00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:17.020
The fact of the matter is, economically speaking, we cannot all verify everything all the time.

355
00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:18.860
It's just not possible.

356
00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:27.680
Like I cannot verify every time I go into a restaurant that the food is good, that it's, you know, if they say it's organic, it's actually organic.

357
00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:35.300
You know, for me to go and interview someone and try to do the testing, et cetera, before I have every meal, or, you know, it's just not possible, right?

358
00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:44.800
Or even to verify that there's no poison in my food or to verify that the plane that I'm just getting onto has been properly serviced.

359
00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:46.860
It's very difficult to verify these things.

360
00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:58.540
So if I had to verify everything without trusting anyone, I would be relegated to living a hermit lifestyle where I would have no interaction with anybody else in the world.

361
00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:01.220
I would have to make my own food.

362
00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:02.840
I would have to have zero trust.

363
00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:06.040
And that's just not who we are as a social species.

364
00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:12.400
So I understand that for sure, particularly in the context when you said in crypto, there's a lot of nonsense hands in crypto.

365
00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:17.460
There's a lot of people that are just in crypto for the wrong reasons, in my opinion, which they're just trying to get rich.

366
00:24:17.660 --> 00:24:23.160
They're trying to screw other people, excuse my language, and steal.

367
00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:24.640
But we can do better.

368
00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:32.840
And I think we need more people to kind of stand up to that and to speak up to that and say, listen, actually, that behavior is not good for society.

369
00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:36.680
Let us work together and where we can serve people.

370
00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:39.740
And where we serve people also is not a bad economic outcome.

371
00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:41.440
You can make money by serving people well.

372
00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:42.280
People like that.

373
00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:43.260
People will come to your platform.

374
00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:44.400
Don't trust verify.

375
00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:44.820
Yes.

376
00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:49.340
You know, that's a good attitude where it's possible.

377
00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:56.180
But it's just practically not possible to lead a full human life without trusting people.

378
00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:57.460
Just it's not possible.

379
00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:59.600
What was your journey into crypto like?

380
00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:03.220
Because I feel like you have this very beautiful view of the world.

381
00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:07.300
And I want to know if that has anything to do with you getting into crypto.

382
00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:08.440
It actually does.

383
00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:12.600
I have a very beautiful friend called Adam.

384
00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:16.100
And he was in the crypto space.

385
00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:21.140
And he had a startup, Chain.com, in 2014.

386
00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.160
I had heard about crypto before that.

387
00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:25.720
I think probably 2012, 2013.

388
00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:26.980
I can't remember exactly what date.

389
00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:28.300
I'd heard about Bitcoin.

390
00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:30.160
I didn't know what it was.

391
00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:31.940
The price had gone up like crazy.

392
00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:32.580
And it crashed.

393
00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:34.040
And I felt this is a scam.

394
00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:35.280
And I don't know what this is.

395
00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:37.160
And, you know, forget about it.

396
00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:38.840
And when the price went down, I felt vindicated.

397
00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:42.760
And then when I heard he was in crypto, he's also a Baha'i.

398
00:25:43.460 --> 00:25:45.500
And I called him up and I said, Adam, what are you doing with your life?

399
00:25:45.700 --> 00:25:46.680
You know, this is beneath you.

400
00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:48.980
You should be doing things that are, you know, more worthy.

401
00:25:49.120 --> 00:25:53.680
And we should be, you know, trying to serve our friends and brothers and sisters around the world.

402
00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:58.800
And he said, you know, I think you don't understand what crypto is or what Bitcoin is and what blockchain is.

403
00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:00.420
So let me explain a few things to you.

404
00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:01.540
And so he did.

405
00:26:02.100 --> 00:26:04.560
And my mind was blown.

406
00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:10.780
Really, just the concept of just things that I didn't even understand about our current monetary system.

407
00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:16.240
And I think that's also one of the problems that is a barrier to people understanding crypto is they don't understand money.

408
00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:17.480
Forget about crypto.

409
00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:18.620
They just don't understand money.

410
00:26:18.700 --> 00:26:27.780
And so they can't understand why crypto and why Bitcoin is such an important concept because they don't understand what the defaults and the deficiencies of money is, what they are.

411
00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:31.260
Once I talked to him, I kind of started thinking about it a lot.

412
00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:34.560
I started, I was working at a bank at the time, started talking to people at the bank.

413
00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:38.340
Ultimately, I became the blockchain lead at the bank for a couple of years.

414
00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:40.280
We built some wonderful stuff in the bank.

415
00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:44.300
We actually built an exchange in the bank, but they didn't want to launch it because it was still very early.

416
00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:48.020
And so that's when my co-founders and I left to start Valor.

417
00:26:49.220 --> 00:27:00.740
And here we are six and a half years later, really just trying to make the world a better place, trying to help build a financial system that recognizes the winneness of the human race and trying to do our best to serve our brothers and sisters around the world.

418
00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:06.600
Okay, so this part of the world, the northeast part of Asia are relatively stable, right?

419
00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:08.840
We have a fairly strong economy.

420
00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:15.520
Of course, people might argue that, oh, our real estate prices are so high and that young people are losing hope they can't afford a house.

421
00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:18.400
This is all true and it's really bad.

422
00:27:18.580 --> 00:27:22.220
But I think there are a lot of places in the world with bigger problems.

423
00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:28.540
And what are some injustices in the financial system that you've seen people dealing with right now?

424
00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:32.100
Absolutely. The first time I had a bank account was in the United States.

425
00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:33.780
I grew up my first 18 years in Kenya.

426
00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:38.640
I didn't have a bank account at that time after a gap year, but I moved to California to start my university education.

427
00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:40.060
And that was the first time I had a bank account.

428
00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:43.520
And I opened my bank account and my banking was free.

429
00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:44.900
I didn't have to pay any fees.

430
00:27:45.160 --> 00:27:48.280
It was a pretty low cost financial experience for me.

431
00:27:48.280 --> 00:28:03.200
When I went to South Africa and a much poorer country, obviously, every single bank account, you had to pay at least five or ten dollars a month to just have a bank account, let alone transact on that bank account.

432
00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:05.400
Because there are more fees with transactions as well.

433
00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:12.940
I remember thinking, gosh, I'm giving my funds to the bank so that the bank can use my funds to lend out and make money on those funds.

434
00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:17.220
And I'm paying the bank for the privilege of then using my money to make money from me.

435
00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:19.220
So it was very perverse.

436
00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:20.160
I thought it was very perverse.

437
00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:25.640
So that was one area where I thought to myself, gosh, this seems a little bit strange.

438
00:28:25.840 --> 00:28:28.000
It has started to change a little bit in South Africa.

439
00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:38.740
Yeah, so talking about injustices, the fact that when in Africa, when you send money from South Africa to Zimbabwe, there's a big remittance corridor.

440
00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:40.760
A lot of Zimbabweans are living in South Africa.

441
00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:41.580
They're sending money back.

442
00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:46.880
Pretty much the standard fee that people are paying for that privilege is 10%.

443
00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:48.060
Wow.

444
00:28:48.260 --> 00:28:49.900
So 10%.

445
00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:54.380
And that's actually not a bad deal when it comes to many other remittance corridors, right?

446
00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:59.100
You can look up the stats, but then oftentimes 18% or 20%.

447
00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:09.940
I think there's a tremendous amount of injustice there where you have the intermediaries that are making a lot of money off the back of a lot of poor people around the world.

448
00:29:10.400 --> 00:29:13.580
And I think that there needs to be a better way.

449
00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:15.900
So that's just one example of the injustice.

450
00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:18.760
Another injustice of our current system, I think, is inflation.

451
00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:20.960
Inflation hits the poor the most.

452
00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:29.680
It's kind of part and parcel of our current system where inflation is embedded into our economic structures, into the mandates of central banks.

453
00:29:30.420 --> 00:29:32.360
I think there's a lot of injustice there.

454
00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:34.500
I often ask myself, why do we even have inflation?

455
00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:40.400
You know, why is inflation, the inflation target 2%, 6%, depending on where you are in the world.

456
00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:43.260
Why isn't it 1% or 0% or negative?

457
00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:47.200
These are just a couple of obvious cases of injustice.

458
00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:51.540
But there are many other cases of injustice around the world.

459
00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:54.120
You know, we can talk about property rights.

460
00:29:54.400 --> 00:30:05.120
We can talk about the fact that we have problems with property rights across Africa and different parts of the world where it's very difficult to start building wealth for yourself.

461
00:30:05.120 --> 00:30:10.260
If you can't even own the place that you're trying to live or have any pathways to owning that.

462
00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:15.100
Or if you do own the place that you live, but it's very difficult to prove that you own it.

463
00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:21.040
And so you can't get credit against the asset that you have to try to build more wealth.

464
00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:27.120
And so there's just a lot of things that are wrong with our financial system and society at large.

465
00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:28.900
I don't have all the answers to them, obviously.

466
00:30:29.800 --> 00:30:31.360
But we're just trying to do our best.

467
00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:35.600
I want to know, okay, do you think central banks should not exist?

468
00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:42.040
Because they're also helping the society by, you know, providing public transportation, healthcare.

469
00:30:42.460 --> 00:30:42.860
I don't know.

470
00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:44.360
Are they doing enough?

471
00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:46.400
Well, I don't think all central bankers are evil.

472
00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:47.180
Let me put it that way.

473
00:30:47.180 --> 00:30:51.140
I think there are many central bankers that are trying to do their best.

474
00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:52.320
They're trying to help society.

475
00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:53.540
They're trying to progress society.

476
00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:57.080
But we're in a system that has a lot of flaws.

477
00:30:57.380 --> 00:31:00.620
And when you're part of that system, it's very difficult to change it from within.

478
00:31:00.980 --> 00:31:02.740
I'll give you one small example.

479
00:31:03.140 --> 00:31:06.860
You know, in South Africa and other places of Africa, there's a lot of poverty.

480
00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:16.520
If you're driving from one place to another, you're going to pass a lot of people that haven't eaten for the last 18 hours, 24 hours, or whatever it is.

481
00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:19.680
Or who are sleeping, who don't have shelter, right?

482
00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:21.180
Or who are homeless, you know?

483
00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:35.980
Our progeny and our children, children, children, when they look back at us, they might look at Farzalm and they're like, Farzalm, you used to drive from your home, the comfort of your home, to the bank where you were working at previously.

484
00:31:36.340 --> 00:31:38.480
And you used to pass poor people.

485
00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:42.540
And you knew they were poor because they were literally asking for money.

486
00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:46.040
You would just pass them and you would go to your work.

487
00:31:46.520 --> 00:31:53.340
You know, how could you continue with your luxurious lifestyle when you knew another human being didn't even have enough to eat?

488
00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:56.420
You just pass them and you're listening to your music or your radio.

489
00:31:56.520 --> 00:32:00.640
And how primitive of a society was that where we didn't take care of our own?

490
00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:05.580
The reason I share that is that, you know, someone could say that, oh, Farzalm, you're evil.

491
00:32:05.580 --> 00:32:10.780
Like, looking back in this, you know, how could you not stop and help another human being, right?

492
00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:11.520
You had the resources.

493
00:32:11.860 --> 00:32:14.680
But our system is such that it's overwhelming.

494
00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:19.520
Like, I can't single-handedly go and solve poverty, right?

495
00:32:19.560 --> 00:32:20.480
Or world poverty.

496
00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:31.100
And so in the same way, I think there is a state we are in systems like the central bankers are in a particular system that I think is very flawed, that I think needs to change fundamentally.

497
00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:36.600
But I don't think many of them are purposefully doing things that are evil.

498
00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:39.640
So I think our systems need to change, right?

499
00:32:39.640 --> 00:32:42.840
But there are many people that are stuck in the system that are doing their very best.

500
00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:45.300
But the system is flawed, not necessarily the people.

501
00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:52.000
I really question a lot when a lot of the colleagues in the crypto space speak about things like that.

502
00:32:52.040 --> 00:32:53.400
Or when they say, oh, bankers are bad.

503
00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:54.460
No, bankers are not bad.

504
00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:58.460
Some bankers may be bad, but there are a lot of people in crypto that are bad as well.

505
00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:02.860
Talking about system, there is this Bitcoin premium in Africa.

506
00:33:03.540 --> 00:33:06.140
And I think some parts of the world do not understand why.

507
00:33:06.140 --> 00:33:12.080
And they're like, oh, it's such a great arbitrage opportunity and you can just easily make a quick buck, right?

508
00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:15.660
But it's actually not as simple as it sounds.

509
00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:18.560
In South Africa, there's a structural arbitrage.

510
00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:21.340
And what that means is there's capital controls.

511
00:33:22.340 --> 00:33:30.620
And what that means is that it's very difficult to get your money out of South Africa sometimes because of various regulations.

512
00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:35.360
There isn't most of the world's mining is not done in South Africa.

513
00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:40.320
When I say mining, I'm talking about Bitcoin mining because there's a lot of other mining that goes on in South Africa.

514
00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:42.960
But I'm talking about Bitcoin mining right now as an example.

515
00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:53.740
And for crypto to be able to be brought into the country, people need to use their exchange control allowances, their capital control allowances,

516
00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:58.860
to be able to buy Bitcoin and then bring it into the country and sell it in the country.

517
00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:06.200
But there's a much higher demand than the amount that's being sold in the country because of a shortage of supply.

518
00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:15.540
And so typically you have the price of Bitcoin ranging in between one and a half to two percent higher in South Africa than it does on international markets.

519
00:34:15.540 --> 00:34:22.300
Like economists and their educated people, they say, oh, you know, but we know that arbitrages get arbitraged down to zero.

520
00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:24.320
And yes, that's what the textbook says.

521
00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:30.980
But the textbook doesn't talk to you about the fact that there's an exchange control regulation that limits the flow of money.

522
00:34:30.980 --> 00:34:42.420
And so that arbitrage exists because of a structural challenge with supply and demand dynamics in the cryptocurrency markets within South Africa.

523
00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:45.860
Something similar exists within Korea.

524
00:34:46.220 --> 00:34:48.060
Something similar exists within Brazil.

525
00:34:48.800 --> 00:34:55.680
Pretty much whenever you have a particular economic system that has some inhibitions of free capital flowing,

526
00:34:55.680 --> 00:35:09.420
then you result in some type of an anomaly of supply and demand that results in either a premium or a discount to the price of crypto or other type of financial assets.

527
00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:11.080
So that's why it exists.

528
00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:20.140
Now, it's not easy to take advantage of it necessarily because the way to take advantage of it is generally by taking money out of that system,

529
00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:22.440
buying and then selling it back into that system.

530
00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:26.520
But if you're from outside of that system and you want to take advantage of it,

531
00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:35.340
then you have to buy, take it out of the system or then sell into the system and then take it out again to repatriate your profits.

532
00:35:35.780 --> 00:35:42.620
But the whole point is that you cannot repatriate your profits in many circumstances, which is why the arbitrage exists.

533
00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:45.500
So there's some peculiarities to it.

534
00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:56.080
Some people have figured out how to take some advantage of it, but it's not something that anybody can just come and take advantage of it because then the textbooks would be right and it would go down to zero.

535
00:35:56.620 --> 00:35:59.220
But it stays around one and a half to two percent.

536
00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:09.780
Is it correct to say that, let's say if I want to do this arbitrage and I go and sell my Bitcoin and I will hold local currency, right?

537
00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:16.260
But it is very hard for me to change that back to U.S. at the same rate or like at the official rate.

538
00:36:16.260 --> 00:36:22.240
That's right. That's right. Or even that you may not be able to take it out of the country or at any rate.

539
00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:26.000
And that's where the challenge comes in because you've got the local currency.

540
00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:30.300
But then if you don't have the right approvals, then you may not be able to take it out.

541
00:36:30.380 --> 00:36:31.380
So that's where the challenge comes.

542
00:36:32.300 --> 00:36:36.580
So it would make sense for the local people to do it, would it?

543
00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:43.440
It does. We used to have a product at Valor where we did this for our customers.

544
00:36:44.180 --> 00:36:50.600
And because there's an arbitrage, arbitrage by definition means kind of risk-free profit, right?

545
00:36:50.600 --> 00:36:58.460
So you're not taking on risk because there's an option to buy and sell in different markets at different prices to kind of lock in a profit.

546
00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:12.260
And so we had to spend a lot of time with our own product to make it not look like it was a scam because it's like you click this button and we do three trades and then you end up with a profit instantaneously.

547
00:37:12.520 --> 00:37:15.360
And it actually looks like this is not possible.

548
00:37:15.540 --> 00:37:24.020
So if you don't understand the dynamics of how it actually happens, then you'd be saying, wait a minute, this doesn't make any sense.

549
00:37:24.020 --> 00:37:28.400
And it's true, there's limits to that. So you can't keep doing that because that's the whole point.

550
00:37:28.460 --> 00:37:32.720
You've got an allowance and then once you hit that allowance, it stops.

551
00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:39.620
But it's a very strange concept if you're not familiar with it about, wait, how does that work and how do you just create money?

552
00:37:40.340 --> 00:37:46.000
But arbitrage is a normal concept in financial markets when there's a divergence of prices in two different markets.

553
00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:49.500
Do you still have that product? Because I would love it.

554
00:37:49.500 --> 00:37:51.820
We don't. Yeah, we don't. We used to.

555
00:37:51.820 --> 00:37:58.840
The reason we don't have it is that one of our banking partners didn't fully understand it.

556
00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:01.940
And they said, listen, we don't know what this is about and we don't like it.

557
00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:07.520
So, you know, for you to continue having your banking relationship with us, you need to not offer this product anymore.

558
00:38:08.020 --> 00:38:13.020
So we try to explain to them and say, listen, this is not there's nothing untoward whatsoever here.

559
00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:18.160
In fact, we are helping people a lot because there's a structural inefficiency in our markets.

560
00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:22.620
And we're actually helping to monetize that for customers where they can do well.

561
00:38:22.940 --> 00:38:24.740
But they didn't seem to understand it.

562
00:38:24.880 --> 00:38:30.240
And so for the sake of holding on to our banking relationship, we discontinued the product.

563
00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:32.600
Oh, no, it's so different.

564
00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:36.760
Like it's not like any product you've heard on any exchanges.

565
00:38:37.620 --> 00:38:39.780
So that is very special.

566
00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:42.040
We talked about street rate, right?

567
00:38:42.500 --> 00:38:44.620
Who decide what the rate should be?

568
00:38:44.920 --> 00:38:47.420
Like, how do people just agree on a rate?

569
00:38:47.660 --> 00:38:49.500
How do we determine what the rate is?

570
00:38:49.900 --> 00:38:53.400
So ultimately it comes down to buyers and sellers, right?

571
00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:56.120
There are people that like let's talk about Bitcoin.

572
00:38:56.560 --> 00:38:57.880
There are people that have Bitcoin.

573
00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:04.040
And when I said it, there are people that have, say, dollars or rands and they want to buy Bitcoin.

574
00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:16.480
And so in a market, you have buyers and sellers in a free market that can come together and basically have a bidding process to see where does the rate kind of land.

575
00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:17.200
And it's through.

576
00:39:17.560 --> 00:39:26.540
And if you go on to our exchange or any other exchange, you can literally see the order book of all the people wanting to buy and what price they want to buy and the people that are wanting to sell and what price they want to sell.

577
00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:30.620
And it moves and where they cross is where the right rate is, right?

578
00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:44.280
But to your point, in some economies, and depending on what asset we're talking about, there are particular vested interests that do not want a free floating exchange rate, right?

579
00:39:44.320 --> 00:39:51.120
With just pure buyers and sellers just, you know, without a free will coming and buying or selling.

580
00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:58.700
And so they will try to effectively put an artificial price on, let's say, a currency.

581
00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:01.520
And we see this in actually many African nations.

582
00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:06.720
There is oftentimes a particular official rate and then the street rate.

583
00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:15.540
But what you'll see over time is that because it's not based on the free market, it's very, very difficult to sustain that over time because there's an imbalance.

584
00:40:15.540 --> 00:40:25.240
And so over time, what you'll see is that you'll see a very big jump in an exchange rate because they really try to keep it at that rate for a very, very long time.

585
00:40:25.400 --> 00:40:29.360
And then ultimately, it jumped up and then it got devalued or something like that.

586
00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:37.780
And so what we were just talking about, arbitrage, is where people can see the price of one product, but in different markets that's different.

587
00:40:38.260 --> 00:40:39.700
They'll buy where it's low.

588
00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:41.500
They'll sell where it's high.

589
00:40:41.860 --> 00:40:43.840
By buying where it's low, they push up the price.

590
00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:45.860
By selling where it's high, they pull down the price.

591
00:40:46.340 --> 00:40:50.420
And by doing that, they try to bring into equilibrium the price of an asset.

592
00:40:50.820 --> 00:41:05.340
And so we're seeing multiples of tons and tons of market participants all around the world in different markets for one particular asset like Bitcoin, trying to figure out what the price is and where their inefficiency is in the market.

593
00:41:05.340 --> 00:41:10.600
And many companies have explicit arbitrage strategies.

594
00:41:10.600 --> 00:41:16.040
This is how they make money to kind of bring efficiencies into the market, to buy where it's low, to sell where it's high.

595
00:41:16.760 --> 00:41:23.000
And those arbitragers actually perform a great service to financial markets because they bring prices in line.

596
00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:28.640
So let me say something that's, again, probably not very consistent with a lot of the crypto community.

597
00:41:28.640 --> 00:41:32.120
There's this saying that, you know, Bitcoin fixes it.

598
00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:40.360
And people say tongue in cheek a lot of the time, but a lot of people believe that, you know, if we just get onto a Bitcoin standard, then the problem is the world will become much better.

599
00:41:41.060 --> 00:41:45.220
I think that's a very, very simplistic view of the complexities of humanity.

600
00:41:45.220 --> 00:41:47.840
And I view Bitcoin as a tool.

601
00:41:48.140 --> 00:41:49.580
I think, is it a better instrument?

602
00:41:49.720 --> 00:41:50.980
I do think so, right?

603
00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:53.660
Can it serve humanity in many ways?

604
00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:54.480
I do think so.

605
00:41:55.020 --> 00:42:07.520
But in and of itself, if we don't change anything else about society, about how we govern ourselves, about how we look at justice, about how we try to solve our difficulties around the world, Bitcoin itself is just a tool.

606
00:42:07.520 --> 00:42:18.520
So there's a lot of things that we need to do to improve the world, to progress civilization, to improve injustices or right injustices.

607
00:42:18.660 --> 00:42:27.820
I'm hopeful that I can use the few days and years that I have on this earth to try to make the world a better place for my children and their children.

608
00:42:28.080 --> 00:42:29.560
I think our priorities are wrong.

609
00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:37.500
You know, many of us are just trying to fill our bank accounts to get onto these lists and get the richest, you know, who's the richest person in the world?

610
00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:44.980
And every day we get one day closer to the day that we're leaving all of that behind, regardless of your belief system.

611
00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:47.520
So we're going to leave all these things behind.

612
00:42:48.300 --> 00:42:57.980
But I think there's just an overemphasis on material wealth in our world right now at the expense of a lot of justices that we could have.

613
00:42:58.060 --> 00:43:03.280
So anyway, I do think some of our focuses or foci are not quite right in society.

614
00:43:03.280 --> 00:43:04.480
And don't get me wrong.

615
00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:05.680
Money is important.

616
00:43:05.980 --> 00:43:06.860
Wealth is important.

617
00:43:07.460 --> 00:43:07.620
Right?

618
00:43:08.100 --> 00:43:11.360
We need, like with physical wealth, you can do things.

619
00:43:11.460 --> 00:43:13.240
There's a lot that you can do to change it.

620
00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:23.880
I think my main point is that the acquisition of wealth has become the objective itself rather than a means to an end.

621
00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:25.360
So wealth is great.

622
00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:27.360
But the question is, what are you doing with that wealth?

623
00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:35.740
If you're just trying to gather wealth just to show people how much of it you have, that's unworthy to me of a human being.

624
00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:46.540
If you're trying to gain wealth so that you can actually deploy that capital to improving people's lives, taking care of people that are sick, educating children in the community, building institutions that serve humanity.

625
00:43:46.540 --> 00:43:46.580
Wonderful.

626
00:43:47.380 --> 00:43:47.780
Wonderful.

627
00:43:47.960 --> 00:43:49.260
Go and get more and more wealth.

628
00:43:49.580 --> 00:43:53.720
So to your point, I think there's this wrong image like either money is good or bad.

629
00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:54.100
No, no.

630
00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:55.740
I think money is great.

631
00:43:56.120 --> 00:43:59.900
But I think it depends on what you're doing with it and how you view it.

632
00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:05.380
And if it's just, as I said, the end in itself, if money is the end in itself, no.

633
00:44:05.660 --> 00:44:07.180
It needs to be a means to an end.

634
00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:07.900
It's a vehicle.

635
00:44:08.040 --> 00:44:08.480
It's a tool.

636
00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:14.180
Do you think for places where US dollar is harder to get, Bitcoin prices are more volatile?

637
00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:23.120
So I think the price in dollars overall kind of stays consistent in dollar terms around the world.

638
00:44:23.240 --> 00:44:28.280
But I think what changes a lot is when you're doing the price in the local currencies.

639
00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:42.160
And because effectively what you get when you talk, let's say take the rand, the Bitcoin price in terms of rands is effectively the combination of the Bitcoin price in terms of dollars and the dollar price in terms of rand.

640
00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:46.160
And you combine those together, then you get the Bitcoin price in terms of rand.

641
00:44:46.260 --> 00:44:53.900
So you get a very volatile crypto asset, which is Bitcoin priced in dollars.

642
00:44:54.280 --> 00:45:00.460
You add to that quite a volatile FX pair, which is dollar rand.

643
00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:04.040
And you put that together into the price of Bitcoin rand.

644
00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:07.540
And that's where you have much more volatility than just Bitcoin dollars.

645
00:45:07.540 --> 00:45:11.240
That's because also the currencies themselves are very volatile.

646
00:45:11.780 --> 00:45:18.100
But that speaks to more the asset that the Bitcoin is being priced in rather than Bitcoin itself.

647
00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:20.880
Bitcoin's volatility is kind of it is what it is.

648
00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:27.600
But when you price it in a more volatile asset, the volatility of Bitcoin in terms of dollars gets eccentriced even further.

649
00:45:27.600 --> 00:45:32.260
Is Bitcoin more widely adopted in Africa versus other continents?

650
00:45:32.560 --> 00:45:36.660
I want to know if things work differently in emerging markets, right?

651
00:45:37.080 --> 00:45:40.980
Do companies actually use USDT to trade, buy things?

652
00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:52.520
We have customers on Valor who have told me directly that they buy either USDT or USDC, but really to hedge against the devaluation of the local currency.

653
00:45:52.520 --> 00:45:59.380
So they would buy some stable coin, dollar stable coin, hold it on Valor until the time that they needed the funds again.

654
00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:03.320
Then they sold it on Valor and then took the proceeds out in rands, as an example.

655
00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:07.760
So that definitely is there for a very legitimate reason.

656
00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:17.600
We have within Africa, I think in different corridors across the continent, a lot of people use stable coins for remittances.

657
00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:23.140
Much easier than figuring out kind of FX flows oftentimes.

658
00:46:23.520 --> 00:46:27.880
Although mobile money is becoming more and more prevalent within Africa.

659
00:46:28.140 --> 00:46:31.020
But still, you're going between different currencies.

660
00:46:31.020 --> 00:46:45.040
So you probably heard of M-PESA in Kenya, which is, you know, the ability to load your effectively telephone number with a certain balance of Kenyan shillings and then to send those Kenyan shillings to another mobile telephone number.

661
00:46:45.480 --> 00:46:46.940
So that's worked very well.

662
00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:54.720
It's been obviously a big kind of case study for the world over the last 15 years or so about how well mobile money worked in Kenya.

663
00:46:55.060 --> 00:46:57.520
It hasn't really scaled to many other countries.

664
00:46:57.520 --> 00:47:04.840
And also when you look at mobile money in different countries in Africa, you have mobile money to nominate in local currency.

665
00:47:05.540 --> 00:47:18.740
So it's not very easy, for example, to take your Kenyan shillings in Kenya that's on your M-PESA account and send it to somebody in South Africa to be received in rands by mobile money.

666
00:47:18.980 --> 00:47:22.360
You know, you've got to go from your Kenyan shillings, somehow get it to the rands.

667
00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:24.060
Oftentimes it goes to the U.S. dollar.

668
00:47:24.580 --> 00:47:26.720
And there is costs associated with all of that.

669
00:47:26.720 --> 00:47:50.320
So I think many people in Africa are looking at stable coins as a way of just being able to transact, holding a much more stable kind of asset that's backed by the U.S. dollar and being able to transfer peer-to-peer oftentimes without going through a lot of intermediaries that cost them money at every intersection of the transaction.

670
00:47:50.320 --> 00:47:53.180
There's not just anecdotal evidence.

671
00:47:53.180 --> 00:48:01.100
We've seen it on the platform where people are buying, as I mentioned, stable coins for the protection of their own value.

672
00:48:01.680 --> 00:48:07.960
And I think more and more people are realizing that our current financial system is expensive and our current financial system is inefficient.

673
00:48:07.960 --> 00:48:21.260
And crypto promises to bring down costs and it promises to bring down the delays of sending value from one person to another or one institution to another institution.

674
00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:22.460
There's a lot of promise.

675
00:48:22.600 --> 00:48:26.040
That's nothing to do with any illicit activity from the crypto space.

676
00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:29.200
A lot of Valor's trading volume is from institutions.

677
00:48:29.700 --> 00:48:30.260
Is that correct?

678
00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:30.800
That's right.

679
00:48:31.060 --> 00:48:31.580
That is correct.

680
00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:37.100
When you say volume, is it more like fiat to USDT or are they trading other tokens?

681
00:48:37.540 --> 00:48:43.000
So we have about 90 different tokens, probably close to maybe 100 now on Valor.

682
00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:51.180
Some of our biggest pairs are our RAND-denominated pairs, but we also offer Bitcoin USDT, Bitcoin USDC pairs.

683
00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:53.000
We offer Solana.

684
00:48:54.060 --> 00:48:57.840
I mean, pretty much anything you'd expect from a big global exchange.

685
00:48:58.040 --> 00:49:06.080
We're the largest trading venue in the world across all global exchanges of cryptocurrency trading in terms of RAND.

686
00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:10.420
So, for example, Binance does exist in South Africa.

687
00:49:10.420 --> 00:49:19.940
There are RAND pairs, but we are probably 10x or more larger than Binance in terms of the trading volume of RAND pairs.

688
00:49:21.400 --> 00:49:25.220
And so that's Bitcoin RAND, that's USDT RAND, etc.

689
00:49:25.820 --> 00:49:29.760
But we're growing more and more in the non-RAND pairs as well.

690
00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:37.380
So with our Bitcoin USDT, as I mentioned, Bitcoin USDC or other crypto to crypto or ETH in terms of stable coins as well.

691
00:49:37.380 --> 00:49:44.940
And then also we've launched recently our Perpetual Futures, and that's been growing very, very quickly as well.

692
00:49:45.240 --> 00:49:46.660
So our volume has been growing there.

693
00:49:47.380 --> 00:49:53.220
So I think while we come from Africa, our aspiration is really to serve the world.

694
00:49:53.220 --> 00:50:05.720
As I mentioned earlier, sitting in Hong Kong right now, we're visiting some of our customers over here, some of our partners, also trying to make new partners in this part of the world.

695
00:50:06.160 --> 00:50:08.980
And I say in this part here and we're going to Singapore as well.

696
00:50:09.400 --> 00:50:13.660
We're the largest in Africa, but our aspirations are much larger to serve the globe.

697
00:50:13.740 --> 00:50:17.540
I see Africa as this land of so much opportunity.

698
00:50:17.540 --> 00:50:20.420
And why do you want to expand to Asia?

699
00:50:20.720 --> 00:50:22.760
We've got a lot of big players here already.

700
00:50:23.200 --> 00:50:26.100
A lot of our customers actually are from East Asia.

701
00:50:26.360 --> 00:50:30.820
We have a lot of also corporations, training firms from East Asia that are on valor.

702
00:50:31.540 --> 00:50:39.780
And so it's a corridor that I think is going to become more and more relevant in the future, particularly with trade that happens between Africa and Asia.

703
00:50:39.780 --> 00:50:45.760
So to your point, there are some great companies, great platforms within Asia.

704
00:50:46.900 --> 00:50:49.720
They don't offer everything we have to offer.

705
00:50:50.500 --> 00:50:55.340
And also, I think that we can do certain things better than they can do.

706
00:50:56.280 --> 00:51:02.300
One of the things that I really mean, it's very difficult to know what goes on behind the scenes of any other crypto exchange.

707
00:51:02.300 --> 00:51:07.420
Obviously, I am behind the scenes for valor as one of the co-founders and the CEO.

708
00:51:08.000 --> 00:51:09.860
So I know that we're doing things properly.

709
00:51:10.220 --> 00:51:12.240
It doesn't mean that there are no risks for the valor, right?

710
00:51:12.280 --> 00:51:20.660
There's always security risk for any cryptocurrency exchange, whether you're Coinbase, Binance, OKEx or anything.

711
00:51:21.060 --> 00:51:22.780
And that exists with valor, too.

712
00:51:22.860 --> 00:51:24.320
So it's not to say that there's no risk.

713
00:51:24.440 --> 00:51:25.440
Risk always exists.

714
00:51:25.440 --> 00:51:34.220
I do know that at valor, we've got a very strong values-based approach to how we operate.

715
00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:37.860
There are lines you just never, ever cross.

716
00:51:38.260 --> 00:51:43.040
And some of those lines have been crossed by other competitors in the past that no longer exist.

717
00:51:43.440 --> 00:51:47.240
I remember telling the team, this is before FTX went down.

718
00:51:47.560 --> 00:51:52.320
I said, guys, we don't know what the future crypto landscape will look like.

719
00:51:52.320 --> 00:51:56.840
All we can do is keep serving people, keep doing the right thing, keep building.

720
00:51:57.040 --> 00:52:00.500
For all we know, next year, FTX or Binance won't be around.

721
00:52:00.680 --> 00:52:01.860
Those are the top two at the time.

722
00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:06.000
Lo and behold, the following year, FTX was not around, as an example, right?

723
00:52:06.160 --> 00:52:13.340
Going back to our earlier part of the conversation, lack of integrity of many operators in our space, right?

724
00:52:13.380 --> 00:52:18.340
And what we want to really bring is really strong integrity, saying, listen, come and trust us.

725
00:52:18.340 --> 00:52:21.000
Do your verification to the extent that you can that we talked about.

726
00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:21.500
Absolutely.

727
00:52:21.500 --> 00:52:25.240
And if you don't like what you see, take your money elsewhere, no problem whatsoever.

728
00:52:25.860 --> 00:52:34.920
But I think we offer something to our customers that have come to know us, which is trust is really priceless, right?

729
00:52:35.120 --> 00:52:37.440
And trustworthiness is really priceless.

730
00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:42.100
So we're here just to kind of provide a platform that we believe is very trustworthy.

731
00:52:42.620 --> 00:52:45.700
And we offer it to our guests around the world.

732
00:52:45.700 --> 00:52:46.820
If they want to come to us, great.

733
00:52:46.900 --> 00:52:49.640
If they don't, we're also happy with that.

734
00:52:49.640 --> 00:52:57.560
We're the only crypto trading platform in the world that offers perpetual futures denominated in rands.

735
00:52:57.980 --> 00:52:58.940
That's just one example.

736
00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:01.940
And a lot of traders in the crypto space like a lot of volatility.

737
00:53:01.940 --> 00:53:12.720
And so we have perpetual futures that are denominated in rand, which means you get the volatility of Bitcoin dollar price and you get the volatility of the dollar rand price in one instrument.

738
00:53:13.100 --> 00:53:17.260
So people that want volatility, they can come and trade that on valor, right?

739
00:53:17.260 --> 00:53:25.440
We also have a fantastic cross margin engine, which means you can come and bring your, you can get credit, right?

740
00:53:25.440 --> 00:53:34.840
If you're trading on margin on valor, you can bring basically collateral in terms of USDC or USDT.

741
00:53:34.840 --> 00:53:37.460
And then you can trade a Bitcoin rand pair.

742
00:53:37.740 --> 00:53:39.660
But with collateral, that has nothing to do with that pair.

743
00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:44.480
Some other platforms do offer that, but we're doing it in a very seamless way.

744
00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:52.240
So our idea and our objective is to really make the trading experience a very simple one for customers across the world.

745
00:53:52.600 --> 00:53:56.260
We believe that we're doing that better than many platforms that exist out there.

746
00:53:56.260 --> 00:54:04.400
So just the experience and some of the product and some of the pairs are some that don't exist on all platforms in the world and some that only exist on valor.

747
00:54:04.400 --> 00:54:08.920
Do you think that would be the selling point for people in Asia to try out the platform?

748
00:54:09.340 --> 00:54:09.880
I think so.

749
00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:11.280
It just depends on who you are.

750
00:54:11.460 --> 00:54:21.320
Like if you're a retail customer in Asia that, you know, you want to buy some crypto, then, you know, a Bitcoin rand pair is not necessarily something that you want to be trading.

751
00:54:21.320 --> 00:54:36.400
If you are an Asian trading firm that's trying to look for instruments around the world that have different correlations to pairs that they're trading, you know, on their other exchanges, it's a fantastic place to come to.

752
00:54:36.820 --> 00:54:36.940
Right.

753
00:54:37.060 --> 00:54:43.300
Which is why I believe we have a lot of customers from different parts of the world that still come and trade on valor.

754
00:54:43.300 --> 00:54:43.780
Right.

755
00:54:44.660 --> 00:54:51.160
And not just the Bitcoin dollar pairs, but Bitcoin rand pairs and other pairs that we all have to offer.

756
00:54:51.320 --> 00:54:54.320
I really always want to do what's best for people.

757
00:54:55.040 --> 00:55:00.340
And so while I think there are a lot of people in the crypto space that say, oh, crypto is the future.

758
00:55:00.440 --> 00:55:02.040
Everybody should get into crypto right now.

759
00:55:02.340 --> 00:55:03.360
I don't say that.

760
00:55:03.480 --> 00:55:14.440
I think crypto, particularly when you come from a continent where there's a lot of poverty and when crypto is so volatile, I explicitly say on my interviews on the radio that crypto is not for everybody at the moment.

761
00:55:14.440 --> 00:55:14.800
Right.

762
00:55:14.800 --> 00:55:27.920
If you're living hand to mouth and you are trying to save for your rent next month, I don't think you should necessarily be putting that money into crypto because it could half between now and the time you need to pay your rent.

763
00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:36.060
So in the same kind of vein, I wouldn't say that what our product is is there for everybody in Asia.

764
00:55:36.300 --> 00:55:42.740
We have products, the stuff that people are trading, Bitcoin dollar and perpetuals.

765
00:55:42.860 --> 00:55:45.400
We have that stuff and that we do compete for things that they want.

766
00:55:45.400 --> 00:55:49.820
But not all of these products that I'm talking about would be relevant to all customers around the world.

767
00:55:50.560 --> 00:55:55.580
So it just depends on on your needs and whether whether it matches those needs or not.

768
00:55:55.960 --> 00:56:02.140
That is interesting because a lot of people in crypto would say, by the way, when I say crypto, I mean Bitcoin.

769
00:56:02.800 --> 00:56:08.060
People would say Bitcoin is a hedge against inflation so that if you're poor, you should get in early.

770
00:56:08.500 --> 00:56:08.660
Yeah.

771
00:56:08.660 --> 00:56:09.020
Yeah.

772
00:56:09.220 --> 00:56:16.000
So, you know, if you talk to many people, that narrative is right over a period of about four years.

773
00:56:16.320 --> 00:56:19.920
In the long run, Bitcoin acts as a very nice hedge against inflation.

774
00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:22.720
In the short run, it's very, very volatile.

775
00:56:23.660 --> 00:56:26.600
And you could actually lose a lot of value in the short run.

776
00:56:27.440 --> 00:56:31.620
And so particularly for people that don't have the disposable income to put away for several years,

777
00:56:31.620 --> 00:56:38.440
but the money that they're earning is needs to be paid off, you know, the next day or the next week.

778
00:56:38.780 --> 00:56:43.100
That narrative, I think, is not consistent with people's reality on the ground.

779
00:56:43.240 --> 00:56:47.140
And so they don't have enough wealth to be able to wait for four years.

780
00:56:47.620 --> 00:56:52.080
So I think that narrative is if you have some money to put away for a few years and not look at it, great.

781
00:56:52.080 --> 00:56:59.200
But if you don't have that money, then you can't be kind of pitching that narrative to someone that's living, you know, hand to mouth on a daily basis.

782
00:56:59.200 --> 00:57:02.340
What is the growth story of Valor?

783
00:57:02.540 --> 00:57:12.800
About two years ago in 2022, we raised $50 million from Pantera, Coinbase, GSR, Avon Ventures,

784
00:57:13.040 --> 00:57:17.920
which was, you know, the investing arm of Fidelity, one of the largest asset managers in the world.

785
00:57:18.100 --> 00:57:19.040
So that was a great privilege.

786
00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:21.300
It was a wonderful milestone for us at Valor.

787
00:57:22.300 --> 00:57:26.160
And just growth-wise, as I said, we've been around for six and a half years.

788
00:57:26.160 --> 00:57:31.520
We have been growing consistently, actually, over that time.

789
00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:38.000
Even in the bear markets, actually, while a lot of people came down in a big way, we kind of plateaued.

790
00:57:38.000 --> 00:57:40.520
We didn't come down, we just kind of stayed consistent.

791
00:57:40.680 --> 00:57:44.680
And we're not picking up again with the recent movement in prices upwards.

792
00:57:45.060 --> 00:57:47.040
We've always been growing as a team.

793
00:57:48.420 --> 00:57:50.320
We are growing in jurisdictions.

794
00:57:50.320 --> 00:57:52.620
We're licensed in South Africa now.

795
00:57:53.080 --> 00:57:57.540
We've got an approval to operate crypto asset services in Poland.

796
00:57:57.860 --> 00:58:00.220
We have initial approval from Dubai.

797
00:58:00.740 --> 00:58:03.240
But there's a lot of luck that is also involved in these things.

798
00:58:03.380 --> 00:58:09.620
You know, I think many founders are very quick to ascribe success to themselves and failure to the market or external elements.

799
00:58:10.300 --> 00:58:12.060
And I think that's not truthful.

800
00:58:12.520 --> 00:58:15.720
So I think we've also been very lucky we've been in the right place at the right time.

801
00:58:16.520 --> 00:58:18.300
So long may that continue.

802
00:58:18.620 --> 00:58:19.420
Yes, Inshallah.

803
00:58:19.420 --> 00:58:20.900
Inshallah, absolutely.

804
00:58:20.980 --> 00:58:22.480
What are African retails like?

805
00:58:22.560 --> 00:58:27.620
Are they in crypto more for the transaction or the investment or gambling?

806
00:58:27.960 --> 00:58:29.980
I think there's a big part that's speculative.

807
00:58:30.420 --> 00:58:37.640
I wouldn't say gambling because I think there's a real problem with the devaluation of local currency.

808
00:58:38.380 --> 00:58:40.540
So a lot of people say, listen, I've got this local currency.

809
00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:48.400
But I think I will speculate and buy some crypto with the aspiration and the hope that it retains its value better than the local currency.

810
00:58:48.400 --> 00:58:49.660
We have a lot of traders.

811
00:58:49.660 --> 00:58:54.180
I'm on a bunch of trading WhatsApp groups and Telegram groups.

812
00:58:54.180 --> 00:58:59.960
And there are a lot of people that are trading for a living, retail traders that do that.

813
00:58:59.960 --> 00:59:06.360
A lot of people that are looking at the charts and doing technical analysis and things of that nature.

814
00:59:06.520 --> 00:59:07.660
People that are buying and holding.

815
00:59:07.660 --> 00:59:10.860
And then there are the traders that are trading.

816
00:59:10.860 --> 00:59:15.880
And then there are people that are also just trying to figure out what this whole asset class is.

817
00:59:16.000 --> 00:59:16.900
They're just dabbling.

818
00:59:17.120 --> 00:59:21.860
They'll come and buy and they'll try to send it to another address and see what that experience looks like.

819
00:59:21.920 --> 00:59:24.760
Go on to a blockchain explorer and try to say, oh, my goodness.

820
00:59:24.760 --> 00:59:27.840
I just sent that from here to another crypto platform.

821
00:59:28.060 --> 00:59:29.500
And I can see that on the internet.

822
00:59:30.040 --> 00:59:31.220
And, oh, wow.

823
00:59:31.360 --> 00:59:32.600
It's very, very transparent.

824
00:59:32.880 --> 00:59:34.600
And so it's a really cool thing.

825
00:59:34.640 --> 00:59:36.100
So I think there are different use cases.

826
00:59:36.240 --> 00:59:39.080
There are also some of our customers that are using it to pay for groceries.

827
00:59:39.600 --> 00:59:42.180
So at Valor, we have something called Valor Pay.

828
00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:43.200
That's free.

829
00:59:43.500 --> 00:59:49.800
You can pay any cell phone number, email address, or Valor Pay ID with any currency that you have on Valor.

830
00:59:49.800 --> 01:00:00.660
And we have integrations with some of the largest supermarkets in South Africa, where you can go and literally pay for your groceries with Bitcoin or with Rand or whatever else you want.

831
01:00:01.120 --> 01:00:02.560
And so there are people that do that as well.

832
01:00:02.860 --> 01:00:04.980
The predominant use case still is speculation.

833
01:00:05.640 --> 01:00:18.840
For crypto to have a more dominant role in payments and in ultimately being units of account to price things in, we're going to have to see much more stability.

834
01:00:18.840 --> 01:00:21.740
But I think that's down the line.

835
01:00:21.940 --> 01:00:25.260
I think we still, as humanity, need to figure out what this asset class is worth.

836
01:00:25.600 --> 01:00:27.920
You cannot price Bitcoin alone.

837
01:00:28.500 --> 01:00:29.460
What's the price of Bitcoin?

838
01:00:29.860 --> 01:00:31.940
It has to be in reference to something else.

839
01:00:32.480 --> 01:00:38.680
It has to be in reference to either USD or Rand or another cryptocurrency or whatever it is.

840
01:00:38.840 --> 01:00:44.420
So right now, whatever you price Bitcoin in, it's very volatile.

841
01:00:44.420 --> 01:00:58.640
And particularly when you have economies that are priced in fiat currency, even though you have inflation, at least there's a little bit more kind of transparency into what your costs may be over the next month or two.

842
01:00:58.640 --> 01:01:06.680
Because, you know, your suppliers are charging you in or giving you a price for the next month or 30 days, whatever it may be.

843
01:01:07.200 --> 01:01:16.420
That's why I think for Bitcoin to become a little bit more prevalent in the world, I think we're going to have to see some crises in our traditional financial system.

844
01:01:16.420 --> 01:01:25.980
And then only once we've seen some crises and a transition will we start to see people start to price things in crypto rather than in a fiat currency.

845
01:01:26.380 --> 01:01:30.780
And again, right now, the US dollar is more stable than Bitcoin.

846
01:01:30.780 --> 01:01:39.660
But if you were living in Zimbabwe 15 years ago, the volatility of your local currency was way more volatile than what Bitcoin is today.

847
01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:45.700
And so there's a role for other monetary units to be used in that society.

848
01:01:46.300 --> 01:01:52.900
So I don't know exactly what it would look like, but I do believe that we're going to have quite a few financial crises in the next couple of decades.

849
01:01:53.120 --> 01:01:58.760
And I think coming out of those crises, we're going to see what role crypto and Bitcoin has to play for the world.

850
01:01:58.760 --> 01:02:01.680
How big of a crisis do you think that has to be?

851
01:02:01.960 --> 01:02:03.720
The US dollar cannot exist forever.

852
01:02:04.200 --> 01:02:06.500
And right now it is the reserve currency of the world.

853
01:02:07.360 --> 01:02:21.620
So if you go from a world where the US dollar exists and it's the dominant currency and it's the reserve asset to a world where it doesn't exist anymore, that transition has to be tumultuous.

854
01:02:22.220 --> 01:02:25.640
I cannot see any other transition that's not tumultuous.

855
01:02:25.640 --> 01:02:34.280
I don't know what it would look like, but I do think that there will be some crises within my lifetime that will be very significant.

856
01:02:35.020 --> 01:02:41.580
And that will kind of reset some of the financial equilibria that we think we have in society.

857
01:02:42.200 --> 01:02:43.140
I don't know what they look like.

858
01:02:43.200 --> 01:02:49.600
And by the way, I don't think that if you have crypto that you're going to be, oh, I'm going to be fine because the rest, everybody else is going through a crisis.

859
01:02:49.760 --> 01:02:53.460
And no, I think it's going to be a crisis for the world and that we're going to have to work together to figure things out.

860
01:02:53.460 --> 01:02:57.880
Things are off tilt in many ways in our financial system and in our global society.

861
01:02:58.620 --> 01:03:03.320
And I think somehow they need to come back into some type of equilibrium.

862
01:03:03.860 --> 01:03:06.280
And I think that there will be some crises to do that.

863
01:03:06.440 --> 01:03:09.900
It's either going to be orderly, but I don't see because of all the vested interests that we talked earlier.

864
01:03:10.460 --> 01:03:12.720
I don't see that happening or it's going to be disorderly.

865
01:03:12.840 --> 01:03:14.600
And I think there'll be a big mess.

866
01:03:15.000 --> 01:03:17.300
But I do think that we have the capacity to fix it.

867
01:03:17.500 --> 01:03:18.560
And I think we will fix it.

868
01:03:18.560 --> 01:03:20.300
And I think we'll move to a better world after that.

869
01:03:20.440 --> 01:03:22.900
When you say you don't think the U.S. dollar will last forever.

870
01:03:23.600 --> 01:03:27.520
Okay, we grew up using U.S. dollar or whatever fiat that we're using.

871
01:03:27.520 --> 01:03:31.220
And it's very hard to imagine a world without U.S. dollar.

872
01:03:31.700 --> 01:03:33.440
Why do you say it's going to come to an end?

873
01:03:33.640 --> 01:03:41.800
You know, if you went back to the Roman times and you talked to people about their coins and you said, you know, we're going to live in a financial system in the future without these coins.

874
01:03:41.800 --> 01:03:43.880
People are like, what are you talking about?

875
01:03:44.140 --> 01:03:47.380
Like, how is that even going to work?

876
01:03:47.880 --> 01:03:56.080
And then if you talk to people about a financial system where the money was not even physical, that 95% of our money is digital, that you cannot even see it.

877
01:03:56.660 --> 01:03:58.920
Like, they would really think that you're off your rock.

878
01:03:59.100 --> 01:04:00.280
Like, that doesn't make any sense.

879
01:04:00.520 --> 01:04:03.420
So that's obviously a couple of thousand years that we're talking about.

880
01:04:03.660 --> 01:04:08.480
The point that I'm trying to make is that change is inevitable.

881
01:04:09.640 --> 01:04:10.740
It really is inevitable.

882
01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:15.980
And we have a difficult time perceiving that as humans when you're in a particular society, in a particular paradigm.

883
01:04:16.540 --> 01:04:20.140
But when paradigm shifts come, they can change very, very quickly.

884
01:04:20.740 --> 01:04:25.040
So, again, I'm not smart enough to understand exactly what that shift will look like.

885
01:04:25.480 --> 01:04:29.640
But I do know enough to know that shifts will happen.

886
01:04:30.080 --> 01:04:32.340
They're not always just small incremental shifts.

887
01:04:32.920 --> 01:04:35.520
They're oftentimes huge shifts.

888
01:04:35.740 --> 01:04:36.520
That's sudden.

889
01:04:36.520 --> 01:04:41.660
And so, that are sudden, that change the nature of power in the world.

890
01:04:41.820 --> 01:04:43.560
That change the nature of wealth in the world.

891
01:04:43.700 --> 01:04:45.920
That change the nature of wealth distribution in the world.

892
01:04:46.040 --> 01:04:47.900
That change the nature of health in the world.

893
01:04:48.220 --> 01:04:50.780
You know, Bonnie, we're on the cusp of a lot of craziness in our world.

894
01:04:51.080 --> 01:04:56.320
Like, you know, there are nuclear powers that are making threats to each other all the time right now.

895
01:04:56.320 --> 01:04:56.640
Right.

896
01:04:56.680 --> 01:04:59.600
We have we've got economic crisis around the world.

897
01:04:59.680 --> 01:05:01.580
We've got literally people bombing each other.

898
01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:06.200
Literally human people, humans bombing each other right now, which is crazy.

899
01:05:06.200 --> 01:05:09.840
And we see more and more conflagration and difficulties.

900
01:05:10.280 --> 01:05:14.360
I don't think this just happens and we just keep going and then we're cool.

901
01:05:15.480 --> 01:05:18.300
I think that this is going to come to a head at some point.

902
01:05:18.680 --> 01:05:20.440
And there's going to be some big event.

903
01:05:21.720 --> 01:05:23.140
Again, I don't know what it looks like.

904
01:05:23.740 --> 01:05:26.180
But and again, I don't mean to scare people.

905
01:05:26.620 --> 01:05:29.260
Like, I'm very optimistic about humanity.

906
01:05:29.260 --> 01:05:35.260
But I don't think that the trajectory that we're on as humanity is a sustainable one.

907
01:05:36.280 --> 01:05:40.280
And I think there'll be something that causes the limbs of humanity to quake.

908
01:05:40.820 --> 01:05:40.960
Right.

909
01:05:41.020 --> 01:05:41.980
I don't know what that will be.

910
01:05:42.300 --> 01:05:44.780
I think there will be tremendous change.

911
01:05:45.340 --> 01:05:48.380
And I think oftentimes tremendous change hurts.

912
01:05:48.820 --> 01:05:53.140
But the transition from one era to another, oftentimes tremendous suffering for humanity.

913
01:05:53.480 --> 01:05:54.960
But we'll see what the future holds.

914
01:05:55.240 --> 01:05:57.640
Bitcoin Maxis will say Bitcoin can fix that, right?

915
01:05:57.640 --> 01:06:01.460
And to hedge against all these chaos, you need Bitcoin.

916
01:06:01.800 --> 01:06:02.540
Do you think that's true?

917
01:06:02.720 --> 01:06:03.720
It has its role.

918
01:06:04.200 --> 01:06:06.820
But I don't think it fixes everything, as I've mentioned.

919
01:06:07.100 --> 01:06:09.560
Our political parties are so divisive right now.

920
01:06:09.560 --> 01:06:14.840
If you just look at the U.S. presidential election right now, as an example, it's so acrimonious.

921
01:06:15.200 --> 01:06:16.260
Bitcoin doesn't fix everything.

922
01:06:16.460 --> 01:06:22.300
Bitcoin can contribute to a solution for an economic system in some way.

923
01:06:22.780 --> 01:06:24.900
But society is very nuanced.

924
01:06:25.300 --> 01:06:27.440
We have a lot of work to do with our social relations.

925
01:06:27.440 --> 01:06:29.720
We have a lot of work to do with our mental health.

926
01:06:30.220 --> 01:06:35.920
We have a lot of work to do with taking care of the elderly as our demographics change.

927
01:06:36.160 --> 01:06:45.140
We have a lot of work to do with solving challenges of disease and poverty and making sure that our environment is healthy and stuff like that.

928
01:06:45.140 --> 01:06:47.780
So it helps for sure.

929
01:06:48.020 --> 01:06:50.620
But it's a very simplistic view to say Bitcoin fixes everything.

930
01:06:50.840 --> 01:06:51.360
It's a tool.

931
01:06:51.780 --> 01:06:52.980
And it's a wonderful tool.

932
01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:53.680
Don't get me wrong.

933
01:06:53.680 --> 01:07:04.420
But I don't think we can be so arrogant as to think that a monetary tool can solve all of the issues that we have as a multifaceted society.

934
01:07:04.420 --> 01:07:07.880
I think that's very simplistic and short-sighted.

935
01:07:07.880 --> 01:07:10.100
I love that you have all these really deep thoughts.

936
01:07:10.100 --> 01:07:14.180
And it takes time to really communicate what you or Valor stand for.

937
01:07:14.300 --> 01:07:18.860
But you're in Asia right now for Token 2049 and all these other events.

938
01:07:18.860 --> 01:07:25.380
It's really hard to just sit down with someone and spend two hours with them to talk about all these hopes and dreams.

939
01:07:25.380 --> 01:07:31.220
Can you share with us some observation you have on Valor's reception in Asia?

940
01:07:31.400 --> 01:07:35.420
So we've been actually very pleasantly surprised by our reception in Asia.

941
01:07:35.420 --> 01:07:41.120
We've got a lot of people that love us a lot in Asia who are welcoming us to Asia.

942
01:07:41.460 --> 01:07:42.840
I think it's testament to our team.

943
01:07:42.980 --> 01:07:47.120
We've got a phenomenal team that's built a beautiful product that I think many Asians love.

944
01:07:48.980 --> 01:07:52.520
But we are still in our infancy with Asia.

945
01:07:52.520 --> 01:08:06.580
I think we've got a long way to go and we've got a lot of work to do to tell the world and Asia about our product and our services and who we are and what our values are and what we're aiming to do and how well we want to serve people.

946
01:08:07.120 --> 01:08:17.760
And I think one of the things about Asia as well is there's a great level of service and a standard of service that is in many ways unparalleled around the world.

947
01:08:17.760 --> 01:08:25.920
And it's something that inspires us and we're very excited to kind of contribute to that level of service that we see in other places in Asia.

948
01:08:26.080 --> 01:08:29.500
So I'm very excited to be here.

949
01:08:29.720 --> 01:08:33.720
I am very excited to have a product that can serve people in this part of the world.

950
01:08:34.100 --> 01:08:44.080
And I'm excited to get more and more Asians onto our platform and to serve them and for them to experiment to see how we can serve them better than whatever options that they currently have right now.

951
01:08:44.080 --> 01:08:56.600
But again, I always tell the team that if we're not the best at what we're trying to do and we don't deserve to serve our customers if they have better options out there.

952
01:08:57.080 --> 01:08:57.520
Right.

953
01:08:57.920 --> 01:09:08.240
So really, we're trying to build the best product that we have so that we can serve people to the best of our ability and to the best of the ability that they have to receive a service.

954
01:09:08.240 --> 01:09:14.660
Earlier, you said that in Asia, there is a great level of service or something along the line.

955
01:09:15.000 --> 01:09:20.320
There is a wonderful, I feel, culture of service in East Asia.

956
01:09:20.660 --> 01:09:26.980
There is a level of humility that I often see in many cultures.

957
01:09:27.160 --> 01:09:34.180
There is a kind of oftentimes a culture of putting others forward, helping them.

958
01:09:34.180 --> 01:09:40.180
And it's not as individualistic as, say, the West and the United States is.

959
01:09:40.620 --> 01:09:43.720
There is more a sense of community, I think, in many ways.

960
01:09:44.260 --> 01:09:47.160
There is a sense of society, of societal obligation.

961
01:09:47.580 --> 01:09:57.860
But I think there is definitely a wonderful sense of, and you can correct me if I'm wrong, but I feel like in certain Asian cultures, there is definitely a sense of social obligation.

962
01:09:57.860 --> 01:10:02.000
That you need to do something to serve the society or the community that you're in.

963
01:10:02.700 --> 01:10:02.840
Right.

964
01:10:04.000 --> 01:10:08.940
And I find that that is oftentimes stronger than what I've seen in some other Western cultures.

965
01:10:09.420 --> 01:10:13.560
Do you think that aligns with Valor's value or what you stand for?

966
01:10:13.660 --> 01:10:16.280
Therefore, it would be an advantage.

967
01:10:16.680 --> 01:10:16.940
Definitely.

968
01:10:16.940 --> 01:10:24.300
I definitely think that there is a resonance with our approach and a lot of the Asian culture.

969
01:10:24.800 --> 01:10:27.100
You know, we also try to be really humble at Valor.

970
01:10:27.420 --> 01:10:30.380
I think that there's the humilities and short supply in the world.

971
01:10:30.720 --> 01:10:34.220
Again, Asia is such a complex space with so many different cultures as well.

972
01:10:34.880 --> 01:10:39.320
But do I think that there's a resonance between Valor and the Asian culture?

973
01:10:39.480 --> 01:10:39.740
Absolutely.

974
01:10:40.080 --> 01:10:42.080
Anything exciting going on at Valor?

975
01:10:42.440 --> 01:10:45.540
So there's a lot that's exciting at Valor, as always.

976
01:10:45.540 --> 01:10:49.460
We're building lots of new products and services.

977
01:10:49.800 --> 01:10:51.980
We are improving our current products and services.

978
01:10:52.660 --> 01:10:57.780
We have a lot of our customers that are API traders that are plugged directly into our application programming interface.

979
01:10:58.260 --> 01:10:59.660
So you have a lot of algorithmic traders.

980
01:10:59.820 --> 01:11:03.400
So we're always trying to improve the performance of our platform, reduce latencies.

981
01:11:03.980 --> 01:11:09.480
We have really a world-class API, which is why some of the sophisticated traders around the world are on Valor.

982
01:11:10.040 --> 01:11:11.580
And so we're always improving that.

983
01:11:11.580 --> 01:11:16.700
We are also improving our ability to get money on and off the platform in fiat currency.

984
01:11:16.700 --> 01:11:23.460
So right now we have services in rand, in dollars, in euros.

985
01:11:23.940 --> 01:11:28.220
And we're going to be expanding that even more in the future.

986
01:11:29.180 --> 01:11:34.880
And then also a whole bunch of different yield products that we're going to be bringing to the market soon.

987
01:11:34.880 --> 01:11:35.320
Right.

988
01:11:35.800 --> 01:11:44.300
We really, and maybe to get a little technical for some of the people that might be interested in this, but we look at finance and value across five pillars.

989
01:11:44.300 --> 01:11:53.180
So if you look at finance, I look at finance to say, when you have anything of value, you need that value to be stored.

990
01:11:53.780 --> 01:11:55.660
You need that value to be transferred.

991
01:11:56.340 --> 01:11:58.860
You need that value to be exchanged from one to another.

992
01:11:59.280 --> 01:12:06.880
You need to be able to provide value in loans and you need the ability to protect value, either through insurance or derivatives or things of that nature.

993
01:12:06.880 --> 01:12:12.360
So really, if you look at financial services, pretty much they fit into those five pillars.

994
01:12:13.020 --> 01:12:20.640
And so what we're trying to do is strengthen what we offer in each of those five pillars so that we can have a really strong financial product for anybody around the world.

995
01:12:21.300 --> 01:12:27.180
And we're doing more and more to kind of deepen our products and services in each of those five pillars.

996
01:12:27.460 --> 01:12:29.360
So there's always lots on the go at Valor.

997
01:12:29.540 --> 01:12:32.420
I saw that there is something called Solana Summer.

998
01:12:32.680 --> 01:12:33.160
What is it?

999
01:12:33.160 --> 01:12:38.200
So Solana Summer, it's a celebration almost of Solana that I've been doing for a number of years.

1000
01:12:38.340 --> 01:12:40.320
And on Valor, we have Solana.

1001
01:12:40.460 --> 01:12:46.340
There's a whole bunch of things that we're going to get rewards into our customers' hands.

1002
01:12:46.580 --> 01:12:48.120
There's activities to be done.

1003
01:12:48.320 --> 01:12:51.920
There's basically just a festive season.

1004
01:12:51.920 --> 01:13:00.540
We have a real sense of value sharing at Valor where if we create value, we want to share it back with our customers.

1005
01:13:00.540 --> 01:13:06.320
So Solana Summer is just one way in which we do that, which is a bunch of different things that are happening on the platform.

1006
01:13:06.780 --> 01:13:14.780
You can come in and really celebrate the Solana ecosystem with us and earn a soul and much more on Solana.

1007
01:13:14.920 --> 01:13:20.640
So it's really an excuse to have a good time to give value back to our customers.

1008
01:13:20.640 --> 01:13:24.500
And it has different kind of permutations on the platform itself.

1009
01:13:24.840 --> 01:13:31.340
But that's also one thing in the grander scheme of what our marketing team is doing with what's called the global treasure hunt.

1010
01:13:31.340 --> 01:13:41.960
And a whole bunch of things to kind of get our customers to integrate with us, to have fun, to have different types of activities that they need to do that they can get rewarded for.

1011
01:13:42.480 --> 01:13:48.520
So there's a whole bunch of things that we're just trying to get our engagement with our customers more and more interesting, more and more fun.

1012
01:13:49.000 --> 01:13:51.360
And trying to gamify a lot of the things on the map.

1013
01:13:51.540 --> 01:13:53.800
Do you have a Chinese version of the app coming?

1014
01:13:54.080 --> 01:13:54.620
We do.

1015
01:13:54.700 --> 01:13:55.460
How did you know that, Bonnie?

1016
01:13:55.700 --> 01:13:58.420
So we do have a Chinese version of the app that's coming.

1017
01:13:58.520 --> 01:13:59.660
We've been working on that as well.

1018
01:13:59.660 --> 01:14:05.260
And again, that's really just to expand our access to a larger part of the market.

1019
01:14:05.900 --> 01:14:14.380
A lot of our customers in different parts of the world, obviously Chinese is a language that's spoken by many billions of people around the world.

1020
01:14:15.060 --> 01:14:23.040
And so it's a way for us to kind of make the app feel more native with a product that would have otherwise perhaps felt more foreign.

1021
01:14:23.240 --> 01:14:25.320
We have employees now in Asia.

1022
01:14:25.320 --> 01:14:31.560
We have people that are working on the app that are Asian, that we want to make the app theirs.

1023
01:14:32.360 --> 01:14:40.800
And so this is one of the ways in which we make an international global platform feel very local by bringing it to you in your own language.

1024
01:14:41.040 --> 01:14:44.820
A lot of retails in Hong Kong and China will be asking this question.

1025
01:14:45.220 --> 01:14:47.780
Can I use Valor in Hong Kong or China?

1026
01:14:47.780 --> 01:14:51.560
So we are not allowed to solicit in many parts of the world.

1027
01:14:51.800 --> 01:14:56.460
It depends on the different regulatory regimes in different parts of the world.

1028
01:14:56.980 --> 01:15:07.920
There are certain places where you've got what's called the reverse solicitation regimes where we are not allowed to come to a particular geography and advertise to tell people to come to us.

1029
01:15:07.920 --> 01:15:13.380
But if people from a particular geography come to Valor and use our services, then there's no problem there.

1030
01:15:13.880 --> 01:15:18.800
So in many of the Asian economies, we don't advertise actively.

1031
01:15:20.020 --> 01:15:25.580
But if some of those Asian economies come to Valor and use our services, we welcome that.

1032
01:15:25.740 --> 01:15:27.760
And that's legal and that's allowed.

1033
01:15:28.320 --> 01:15:31.380
The next question is for every crypto holder.

1034
01:15:31.840 --> 01:15:36.300
So you believe in the four-year cycle and can you give us a Bitcoin price prediction?

1035
01:15:36.300 --> 01:15:41.540
The way I like to look at it is by comparing it to something that we know, which is gold.

1036
01:15:42.060 --> 01:15:56.640
Like gold is, depending on which kind of reports you look at, the price of gold altogether is probably between, or the value of all gold that exists right now in the world is anywhere between, let's say, 12 to 17 trillion US dollars.

1037
01:15:56.640 --> 01:16:09.840
Anything that has digital, that has gone on to replace its analog version, has not just gone to match the value of that analog version, but has gone and exceeded it by many orders of magnitude.

1038
01:16:09.840 --> 01:16:20.740
And if you think about mail, like emails, replacing the postal system, not completely replacing it, but the amount of emails that there are way more than there was ever post, right?

1039
01:16:20.860 --> 01:16:30.200
Or the amount of music there's been and people listening to music when it's gone digital or the amount of videos or documents that exist in the world, etc.

1040
01:16:30.200 --> 01:16:36.040
So I do view Bitcoin as a kind of a gold 2.0.

1041
01:16:36.040 --> 01:16:43.080
And I do think that Bitcoin will not go and only hit parity with gold, but I think it will surpass it.

1042
01:16:43.340 --> 01:16:56.040
By my last calculations, if you looked at, if the price of Bitcoin or the value of Bitcoin and the value of gold hit parity, then you're looking at a price of Bitcoin per coin of about 700,000 US dollars.

1043
01:16:56.040 --> 01:17:03.980
And if you look at 700,000 US dollars that just hits parity with gold, I think ultimately we're going to hit higher than that, right?

1044
01:17:04.200 --> 01:17:10.620
But again, this is also a story about the relative valuation of the US dollar versus Bitcoin.

1045
01:17:10.880 --> 01:17:16.600
The price of Bitcoin is extremely low right now compared to what it will be in the future.

1046
01:17:18.060 --> 01:17:24.140
I suppose my price target over the next five to 10 years is probably, I would say, $700,000 per Bitcoin.

1047
01:17:24.140 --> 01:17:28.060
I think that's going to be met relatively easily.

1048
01:17:28.240 --> 01:17:29.700
This is going to be in the highlight.

1049
01:17:30.060 --> 01:17:30.440
All right.

1050
01:17:30.780 --> 01:17:41.920
It sounds like you believe in the God candle or the Omega candle instead of the four-year cycle, because within the four-year cycle, it's more like happening, you know, steady growth and all that.

1051
01:17:42.020 --> 01:17:44.980
But this is more like chaos theory.

1052
01:17:45.180 --> 01:17:46.300
I would say somewhere in between.

1053
01:17:46.300 --> 01:17:48.820
I think there's going to be some big candles, obviously.

1054
01:17:48.820 --> 01:17:57.620
But I think within the four-year cycle, I think, you know, we have seen historically that it kind of goes up and down and up and down, but it's an upward trajectory.

1055
01:17:58.640 --> 01:18:03.820
And so I do view these cycles, and I think there will continue to be cycles, but I think the trajectory is upwards.

1056
01:18:04.380 --> 01:18:11.220
I don't spend too much time thinking about the ups and the downs, like in any particular small moment of time.

1057
01:18:11.220 --> 01:18:16.600
But I do think that the cycles will happen, the volatility will continue, but the trajectory will be upwards.

1058
01:18:16.940 --> 01:18:18.560
Do you believe in altcoins then?

1059
01:18:18.800 --> 01:18:20.300
Because we talk about Bitcoin a lot.

1060
01:18:20.820 --> 01:18:24.740
I think a lot of the crypto assets that exist today will go to zero.

1061
01:18:25.060 --> 01:18:29.840
I don't think there will be space in the future for all of these different coins.

1062
01:18:29.840 --> 01:18:35.300
I think the whole point and my whole thesis is that the financial system of the world needs to get more simple.

1063
01:18:35.420 --> 01:18:39.780
More simple, I'm talking about not the technology, like I'm not a technically inclined person.

1064
01:18:39.880 --> 01:18:40.380
I'm an economist.

1065
01:18:41.060 --> 01:18:44.960
But it works, and it's simple from the perspective of a user experience.

1066
01:18:45.340 --> 01:18:49.800
There will be a situation that is akin to what we saw with the dot-com era.

1067
01:18:49.980 --> 01:18:58.020
But today, some of the most valuable assets in the world are those assets that were born in that era.

1068
01:18:58.020 --> 01:19:05.820
And so I think in the future, many of the most valuable assets in the world will be assets that were born out of this Cambrian explosion of crypto assets.

1069
01:19:06.420 --> 01:19:11.160
But I think many will die along the way, and that will be left to the few that are very, very valuable.

1070
01:19:11.600 --> 01:19:14.960
With that being said, do you think altcoin season will be coming this cycle?

1071
01:19:15.300 --> 01:19:18.940
Because it feels like there are so many coins on the market, and it's all diluted.

1072
01:19:19.340 --> 01:19:21.180
I do think that there will be an altcoin season.

1073
01:19:21.500 --> 01:19:27.980
I think we're still in the early stages of the experimentation that is crypto at the moment.

1074
01:19:28.020 --> 01:19:37.260
And remember, I talk a lot about Bitcoin right now, because that's kind of what's the dominant asset right now within the crypto space.

1075
01:19:37.760 --> 01:19:41.060
But I think, again, one has to have the humility to think, is it going to be Bitcoin?

1076
01:19:41.220 --> 01:19:42.200
Is it going to be something else?

1077
01:19:42.260 --> 01:19:43.760
Is it something that doesn't exist yet?

1078
01:19:43.800 --> 01:19:44.380
I don't know.

1079
01:19:44.860 --> 01:19:53.100
So just as a broader theory, I think that there is going to be an asset or a couple of assets that are going to be worth a lot.

1080
01:19:53.680 --> 01:19:54.660
Maybe Bitcoin, maybe not.

1081
01:19:54.720 --> 01:19:55.140
I don't know.

1082
01:19:55.260 --> 01:20:00.060
But it doesn't make sense to me to have all of these assets worth a lot.

1083
01:20:00.160 --> 01:20:01.840
I think a lot of them will go to zero.

1084
01:20:02.340 --> 01:20:04.240
But is that going to happen tomorrow?

1085
01:20:04.400 --> 01:20:04.640
No.

1086
01:20:04.800 --> 01:20:06.160
I think there's going to be a journey.

1087
01:20:06.640 --> 01:20:10.420
And so altcoin season, I think we'll probably see more of that to come.

1088
01:20:10.820 --> 01:20:14.500
Is Bitcoin spot ETF and Ethereum spot ETF good or bad?

1089
01:20:14.500 --> 01:20:25.060
I think it's good from the perspective of assets that are prevalent in society, make their way into financial instruments like ETF.

1090
01:20:25.660 --> 01:20:36.880
I think there are some people that say that's not such a good thing for society in that it reverts a lot of the kind of inherent attributes of what Bitcoin is.

1091
01:20:37.060 --> 01:20:37.460
Yeah, sure.

1092
01:20:37.680 --> 01:20:38.940
I agree with that, too.

1093
01:20:38.940 --> 01:20:45.860
But if people want to hold it in a vehicle that's an ETF, more power to them, I think that people should have choice.

1094
01:20:46.480 --> 01:20:51.300
And I think it's a natural evolution of where we've come from and where we're going.

1095
01:20:51.800 --> 01:20:56.280
I think there'll be more traditional financial instruments to do with crypto assets as we go forward.

1096
01:20:56.280 --> 01:21:07.460
And I also think that question often comes from people where they're saying, OK, you know, it's the Bitcoin people or the crypto people versus the bankers.

1097
01:21:07.800 --> 01:21:18.300
And I have a view that there will be a merging of these worlds over time and that banks will be offering crypto asset services and crypto asset providers will be offering banking services.

1098
01:21:18.300 --> 01:21:26.220
And that there'll be a consolidation of these different types of services into the kind of financial institutions of the future.

1099
01:21:26.860 --> 01:21:30.620
So I don't think that dichotomy is a false dichotomy.

1100
01:21:31.060 --> 01:21:34.860
And I think that we'll start seeing crypto being integrated.

1101
01:21:35.120 --> 01:21:39.140
And soon, I think we'll be talking about crypto as traditional finance.

1102
01:21:39.460 --> 01:21:40.780
You're based in Johannesburg.

1103
01:21:40.900 --> 01:21:42.780
So that headquarters is Johannesburg, yes.

1104
01:21:42.840 --> 01:21:46.260
OK, you're expanding to the world or is it just Asia for now?

1105
01:21:46.260 --> 01:21:50.240
You know, we are obviously an Internet company.

1106
01:21:50.760 --> 01:21:55.500
So we exist globally on the Internet where we can exist.

1107
01:21:56.060 --> 01:22:01.040
There are certain places where we don't serve the population for regulatory reasons.

1108
01:22:01.520 --> 01:22:03.580
So we are expanding globally.

1109
01:22:03.900 --> 01:22:07.560
But as far as a strategic imperative right now, we're looking at Asia.

1110
01:22:07.780 --> 01:22:11.960
We're looking at Europe as some of the main areas that we're going into.

1111
01:22:11.960 --> 01:22:14.880
There's an emphasis of where we're going proactively.

1112
01:22:15.140 --> 01:22:18.200
But hopefully we get our word out about who we are to the world.

1113
01:22:18.720 --> 01:22:20.660
And we welcome people from all parts of the world.

1114
01:22:20.860 --> 01:22:26.800
And then we'll also continually reflect on where we are, where we see our services are being most used.

1115
01:22:27.200 --> 01:22:31.980
Will we need to kind of pivot and start focusing on different parts of the world?

1116
01:22:32.860 --> 01:22:34.660
That always happens in business.

1117
01:22:34.820 --> 01:22:37.840
You need to be open to kind of moving and adapting to your reality.

1118
01:22:37.840 --> 01:22:41.880
I think people still consider this early stage of crypto.

1119
01:22:42.160 --> 01:22:43.880
And a lot of people are still not in crypto.

1120
01:22:44.020 --> 01:22:47.400
I feel like the majority of the world is still unfamiliar with crypto.

1121
01:22:48.140 --> 01:22:53.940
Any words of encouragement for people that are new to the market or are just discovering this magical world?

1122
01:22:54.340 --> 01:23:01.920
I think what I would say is I really encourage people to be a bit more humble with their approach to something that's new.

1123
01:23:01.920 --> 01:23:13.640
Because everybody comes at it with a preconceived notion of what it is, a preconceived notion that they already understand it or that a particular narrative is correct.

1124
01:23:14.520 --> 01:23:27.060
And so I really propose that people come with an open mind and also question their understanding of what money is, the money that they know of, the money that they use every day, not crypto.

1125
01:23:27.060 --> 01:23:33.080
And once you come with an open mind, then the world of possibilities opens up and you start learning so much.

1126
01:23:33.220 --> 01:23:41.680
And then you can determine what this asset class can do to you or what it can do for the world with a much more open mind to be able to see what reality is.

1127
01:23:41.960 --> 01:23:44.180
I think oftentimes knowledge can be a barrier.

1128
01:23:44.180 --> 01:23:56.320
Because you think you know, but actually you are so, your mind is so puny and what you actually understand is, it's just a very small sliver of what reality is.

1129
01:23:56.520 --> 01:24:02.040
And that you have the arrogance to think that you are all knowing and that you miss out so much.

1130
01:24:02.040 --> 01:24:10.640
So I think sometimes you need to put your own knowledge, put it aside to kind of just look with fresh eyes to see what makes sense and what doesn't.

1131
01:24:11.340 --> 01:24:14.300
And oftentimes you see a lot of magic when you kind of achieve that.

1132
01:24:14.600 --> 01:24:15.800
Do you think we're too late?

1133
01:24:16.320 --> 01:24:18.240
Or if a person's joining now?

1134
01:24:19.340 --> 01:24:19.580
No.

1135
01:24:20.400 --> 01:24:21.140
It is so early.

1136
01:24:21.880 --> 01:24:22.540
It is so early.

1137
01:24:23.040 --> 01:24:24.500
I really think it is so early.

1138
01:24:24.820 --> 01:24:27.260
A lot of what's happening right now is speculation.

1139
01:24:27.260 --> 01:24:36.880
But crypto hasn't impacted the world anywhere close to the possibility that it has to affect tremendous transformation in our financial system globally.

1140
01:24:37.040 --> 01:24:43.580
If the narrative is right, that there's going to be a tremendous change and the world's financial system is going to move and have a paradigm shift.

1141
01:24:44.040 --> 01:24:47.020
But it's one or the, it's not going to stay where it is right now.

1142
01:24:47.040 --> 01:24:47.500
That's for sure.

1143
01:24:47.980 --> 01:24:49.780
So we're either very, very early.

1144
01:24:50.580 --> 01:24:54.320
And we're going to go like north in a very big way.

1145
01:24:55.720 --> 01:24:56.900
Or this is going to collapse.

1146
01:24:57.260 --> 01:24:58.580
But it's not going to be chilling.

1147
01:24:59.080 --> 01:25:01.440
Like the price of Bitcoin is not going to be chilling at $60,000.

1148
01:25:02.500 --> 01:25:03.200
That's for sure.

1149
01:25:04.060 --> 01:25:05.260
So it's all or nothing.

1150
01:25:05.700 --> 01:25:07.320
I think it is progress or regress.

1151
01:25:08.380 --> 01:25:12.940
So I think we're either going to progress or we're going to go forward or we're going to go backward.

1152
01:25:13.100 --> 01:25:18.220
But we're not going to be, we're not going to be just maintaining where we are right now.

1153
01:25:18.220 --> 01:25:20.780
It's just, that's not what life is about.

1154
01:25:21.020 --> 01:25:21.920
Any last words?

1155
01:25:22.160 --> 01:25:23.120
I think we covered a lot.

1156
01:25:23.120 --> 01:25:24.480
Thank you very much for the conversation.

1157
01:25:24.620 --> 01:25:25.220
I really enjoyed it.

1158
01:25:25.220 --> 01:25:39.140
And I think, yeah, the only thing I would just invite people, more and more people to kind of question what they're doing, if they're trying to help advance society, make the world a little bit of a better place.

1159
01:25:39.140 --> 01:25:41.420
I think we need a lot more love in the world.

1160
01:25:41.740 --> 01:25:46.640
I think we need a spirit of camaraderie in a very divided world at the moment.

1161
01:25:47.120 --> 01:25:48.320
We talked a lot about unity.

1162
01:25:48.760 --> 01:25:52.140
And I really hope people will join me in trying to kind of make that vision reality.

1163
01:25:52.400 --> 01:25:55.300
But most of all, thank you to you for a wonderful conversation.

1164
01:25:55.480 --> 01:25:58.060
I really enjoyed meeting you and chatting to you.

1165
01:25:58.100 --> 01:25:59.000
So thank you for your time.

1168
01:25:59.780 --> 01:26:02.220
was amazing talking to you. See you in South Africa.

1169
01:26:02.700 --> 01:26:04.300
I'm looking forward to it. Thanks so much, Bunny.
